---
/*
 * cbf-lib-cost-table — the vendor Line Item Budget (LIB) entry grid.
 * The LIB tab of a CBFish contract (/lib-entry).
 *
 * ── THE DESIGN CLAIM ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A LIB is a WEB OF CROSS-LINE DEPENDENCIES, not a list of independent rows:
 * fringe is a percentage of a salary line, a cell-phone line's months are LINKED
 * to a position's staffing months, indirect is a percentage of the sum of
 * whatever the rate is scoped to. A grid can hold that web — you can see the
 * salary line and everything that follows it at once. A one-line-at-a-time modal
 * fights it. So THERE IS NO ADD-LINE MODAL HERE: "Add line" appends an editable
 * row inline, of the shape the vendor picked, focused and ready to type.
 *
 * ── EVERY CATEGORY CAPTIONS ITS OWN COLUMNS ─────────────────────────────────
 * There is no global <thead>. One header for nine categories forced every
 * section to answer to the same three words — and "Quantity" means months in
 * Personnel, nights in a lodging line, units in Supplies, and nothing at all in
 * Fringe. So each category emits its own caption row under its heading, in its
 * own vocabulary (`columns` in src/data/lib-entry.mjs). Only the three columns
 * that DON'T move keep one name throughout — Amount, Work element and the row
 * action — because the money column has to line up with the sub-block subtotals,
 * the indirect base and the grand total in the table foot.
 *
 * ── THE FIVE LINE SHAPES — the ROW decides its cells, not the category ───────
 * Several categories mix shapes (Vehicles carries B and A; Supplies carries A
 * and D), so the shape is per-row and each row renders only the cells its shape
 * actually has. A collapsed cell is an em dash or a spanned cell — never a dead
 * input.
 *   A  qty-rate       label · qty (+ optional 2nd qty) · unit · cost per unit.
 *                     The vendor TYPES the rate. amount = qty × qty2 × rate.
 *   B  authority-rate label · GSA location + season · qty (+ 2nd qty). The rate
 *                     is REFERENCED from the GSA schedule, rendered as a value
 *                     with its source, not as an open input. A vendor may
 *                     override it; an override above `ceiling` is the failure.
 *   C  pct-of-base    pick a base, enter a percentage. NO qty, NO unit. The row
 *                     shows WHAT it is a percentage of and the resolved base
 *                     dollars, so the dependency is visible on the row itself.
 *   D  lump           label · one dollar amount. No qty, no rate.
 *   E  travel-line    belongs to a TRIP and picks a travel KIND. Nothing about
 *                     it is free text: the kind fixes the unit and the slice of
 *                     the schedule the rate may come from, and the trip supplies
 *                     the locality, the travelers and the occurrence count.
 *                     amount = occurrences × count × duration × rate.
 *
 * ── TRIPS, AND WHY TRAVEL IS THE ONE CATEGORY WITH ITS OWN SHAPE ─────────────
 * Travel is the most RULE-BOUND category in a LIB and was being entered as the
 * least structured: a generic "3 persons × 10 days" typed into two free-text
 * quantity boxes, against a schedule row nothing stopped you picking wrong. Now
 * a trip is a sub-block — the same machinery Supplies uses for Office/Field —
 * carrying destination, purpose, window, travelers and `occurrences`. That last
 * one is what the flat list could not say at all: a semiannual coordination
 * meeting is ONE trip that happens twice, not two rows a reviewer has to
 * recognise as the same journey. Change the trip and every line under it moves,
 * which is the same cross-line dependency the rest of this grid is built around.
 *
 * ── THE LINE'S IDENTITY IS A CONTROL, NOT A TEXTBOX ──────────────────────────
 * Four categories know what a line is called better than the vendor does, so
 * they replace the free-text description: Personnel picks a position off the
 * roster, Travel picks a kind, Vehicles picks a kind, and Fringe names itself
 * after the salary line it follows. All five identity variants ship on every row
 * and one is revealed — which is what keeps a CLONED row correct when it is
 * added to, or moved into, a different category.
 *
 * ── ARITHMETIC IS NEVER DONE HERE ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every amount, subtotal, indirect base and total comes from
 * `priceLines(lines, cfg)` in src/data/lib-entry.mjs, which resolves the
 * dependency order (linked quantities → A/B/D → C → indirect). It is re-called
 * in full after EVERY edit, so a dependent line can never silently disagree with
 * its base. `dependentsOf(id)` names exactly the rows that just moved because of
 * an edit, and they get a brief tint — the money moment of the prototype.
 *
 * ── GATES, RENDERED IN BOTH STATES ON LOAD ──────────────────────────────────
 *   · gsa-over-ceiling — trv-4 Boise lodging overrides GSA at $172 against a
 *     $155 ceiling: danger badge + esa-field-error naming ceiling and overage.
 *   · matches-schedule — every other schedule-rated line renders the PASSING
 *     side of the same gate ("Matches GSA schedule"), so it is legible either way.
 *   · no-allocation — sal-4 Administrative Specialist reads "Not allocated" in
 *     the warning tone with an esa-field-error. Added rows start `allocations:{}`
 *     — correctly unallocated, which moves the gate the moment a line is added.
 *   · capitalization threshold — tested in BOTH directions and shown failing on
 *     load: sup-f4, a $6,200 trap assembly, is filed under Supplies and belongs
 *     in Capital Equipment. The failing row offers the MOVE, not just a verdict,
 *     because the item is correct — only its category is wrong, and retyping it
 *     elsewhere would lose its work-element split.
 *   · contract period — every salary line tests its months against the
 *     12-month period, passing on load and failing live past it.
 *
 * ── EVENT CONTRACT (all on `document`) ──────────────────────────────────────
 * DISPATCH 'cbf-lib:totals' — on mount and after EVERY recompute
 *   detail: { total, direct, indirect, indirectBase, remaining, pctOfValue,
 *             failing, unallocated, capitalFailing, monthsFailing }
 *   failing        = gsaFailingLines(lines).length
 *   unallocated    = unallocatedLines(lines).length
 *   capitalFailing = thresholdMisfiled(lines).length     // both directions
 *   monthsFailing  = monthsOverPeriod(lines).length
 * DISPATCH 'cbf-lib:open-subcontract' — a subcontracts row's jump-out
 *   detail: { sheetId, lineId }        // sheetId = line.sheet
 * LISTEN   'cbf-lib:indirect-config' — a sibling owns the overhead block,
 *   including WHICH categories the rate applies to.
 *   detail: { config }                 // shape of indirectConfig
 *   On receipt: re-run priceLines(lines, config), repaint the indirect base,
 *   indirect and total (the base visibly moves as the scope changes), and
 *   re-fire 'cbf-lib:totals'.
 *
 * bcn-lego-checked: the house rule points data tables at AG Grid, but that rule
 * governs SCANNABLE data grids with search / filter / download chrome. This is an
 * ENTRY FORM — a live control and its own validation state in most cells, with
 * per-row shape branching and colspan collapse — so a semantic <table> carrying
 * esa-* controls is both correct and far lighter. Same call cbf-baselines-table
 * .astro and cbf-lib-pi-documents.astro already made in this spoke; the full
 * ecology catalog was re-checked and has no table/grid lego at all. Everything
 * that ISN'T the grid itself is a lego: entry cells = esa-text-field, GSA row and
 * percentage base = esa-select, add-line = esa-button / esa-dropdown-menu (shape
 * picker), allocation editor = esa-popover, row delete = esa-icon-button,
 * validation = esa-field-error, gate verdicts = esa-badge. Beacon is not cloned
 * on this machine.
 */
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import EsaBadge from '@esa/ecology/esa-badge.astro';
import EsaFieldError from '@esa/ecology/esa-field-error.astro';
import EsaIconButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-icon-button.astro';
import {
  SHAPES,
  categories,
  costLines,
  priceLines,
  effectiveRate,
  effectiveQty,
  baseAmountFor,
  baseLabelFor,
  gsaRate,
  isScheduleRated,
  workElements,
  weLabel,
  contract,
  contractMonths,
  positions,
  positionTitle,
  tripOf,
  TRAVEL_KINDS,
  travelKind,
  travelChain,
  travelCount,
  travelLabel,
  localityNote,
  fringeLabel,
  VEHICLE_KINDS,
  vehicleKind,
  perItemCost,
  thresholdTargetFor,
  countOf,
  indirectConfig,
  usd,
  pct,
  qtyFmt,
} from '../../data/lib-entry.mjs';
import {
  SEVERITIES,
  severityMeta,
  reasonLabel,
  seedReview,
  flagFor,
  isIssued,
} from '../../data/lib-review.mjs';

interface Props {
  /** Section heading above the grid. Pass null to let the page own the heading. */
  heading?: string | null;
  /** One line of context under the heading. Pass null to omit. */
  lede?: string | null;
}
const {
  heading = 'Line item budget',
  lede = `${contract.number} · ${contract.fiscalYear} · ${contract.periodStart}–${contract.periodEnd}`,
} = Astro.props;

const lines: any[] = costLines;
const priced = priceLines(lines, indirectConfig);
const amt = (id: string): number => priced.amounts[id] ?? 0;

/* ── The COR's review layer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Server-rendered from the seeded review, then kept live by the script. A mark
   belongs ON the line it is about, so it renders inside the cost-line cell —
   under the description it disputes — rather than in a list somewhere else. */
const review = seedReview();
const flagOf = (l: any) => flagFor(review, l.id);
const reviewIssued = isIssued(review);

/* The four shapes are a MODEL concept, not vendor-facing copy — a vendor never
   thinks "this is a shape-A line". The shape shows itself through which cells the
   row exposes; naming it on every row was internal vocabulary leaking into the UI
   (and it pushed the Rate-source control a line below its neighbours). Removed. */

/* ── Money / quantity text ── */
const cents = (n: number) => Math.abs((n ?? 0) % 1) > 0.0001;
const money = (n: number) => usd(n, { cents: cents(n) });
/* Field values keep thousands separators (parsed back out on edit) and are never
   rewritten while the vendor types, so the caret can't jump. */
const numText = (n: number | null | undefined) =>
  n == null ? '' : cents(n) ? String(n) : n.toLocaleString('en-US');

/* ── Linked quantity (the cell-phone line follows a position) ── */
const labelOfId = (id: string) => lines.find((l) => l.id === id)?.label ?? id;

/* ── GSA gate ── */
const gsaOf = (l: any) => gsaRate(l.gsaKey);
/* Any SCHEDULE-RATED line can be overridden — shape B, and the GSA-rated travel
   kinds. Keying this to shape B alone left an overridden travel line showing
   "Matches GSA schedule" and "Over GSA ceiling" at the same time. */
const overridden = (l: any) => isScheduleRated(l) && l.rateOverride != null;
const gsaOver = (l: any) => {
  const g = gsaOf(l);
  return !!(g && l.rateOverride != null && l.rateOverride > g.ceiling);
};
const gsaSrcText = (l: any) => {
  const g = gsaOf(l);
  /* The dropdown above already NAMES the schedule row (location · kind · season).
     Restating it here said the same string twice and explained nothing, so this
     line says what the control DOES instead: the rate is referenced, not typed,
     and the schedule sets the ceiling the line is checked against. */
  return g
    ? `Rate referenced from this GSA row — not typed. Ceiling ${money(g.ceiling)}.`
    : 'Pick the GSA schedule row this line references — it sets the rate.';
};
const gsaErrText = (l: any) => {
  const g = gsaOf(l);
  if (!g) return 'Select the GSA schedule row this line references.';
  return `${money(l.rateOverride)} is over the ${g.kind} ceiling of ${money(g.ceiling)} for ${g.location} (${g.season}) — ${money(l.rateOverride - g.ceiling)} over.`;
};
const gsaLabel = (l: any) => {
  const g = gsaOf(l);
  return g ? `${g.location} · ${g.kind} · ${g.season}` : 'Select a schedule row';
};

/* ── Capitalization threshold — a gate that runs in BOTH directions ─────────
   The old grid only tested capital lines for being too cheap. The same threshold
   equally condemns a $6,200 trap assembly sitting in Supplies, so the test now
   applies to both categories and the failing row offers the move rather than
   just naming the problem. */
const onThreshold = (l: any) => l.category === 'capital-equipment' || l.category === 'supplies';
/* Only a per-unit line has a per-item cost to test — a lump has no unit count. */
const thresholdTested = (l: any) => onThreshold(l) && l.shape === SHAPES.QTY_RATE;
const isCapital = (l: any) => l.category === 'capital-equipment';
const perItem = (l: any) => perItemCost(l, priced.amounts);
const capPass = (l: any) =>
  !thresholdTested(l) ||
  (isCapital(l)
    ? perItem(l) >= contract.capitalizationThreshold
    : perItem(l) < contract.capitalizationThreshold);
const capText = (l: any) =>
  `${money(perItem(l))} per item · threshold ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)}`;
const capErrText = (l: any) =>
  isCapital(l)
    ? `${money(perItem(l))} per item is below the ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} capitalization threshold — this item belongs in Supplies & Equipment.`
    : `${money(perItem(l))} per item is at or above the ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} capitalization threshold — this item belongs in Capital Equipment.`;
const capPassText = (l: any) =>
  isCapital(l) ? 'Meets capitalization threshold' : 'Below capitalization threshold';
const capFailText = (l: any) =>
  isCapital(l) ? 'Below capitalization threshold' : 'Over capitalization threshold';
const capMoveText = (l: any) =>
  isCapital(l) ? 'Move to Supplies & Equipment' : 'Move to Capital Equipment';

/* ── Contract-period guard on staffing months ───────────────────────────────
   Budgeting a position for more months than the contract runs is arithmetic the
   form can check, and it was being left to a reviewer. */
const monthsTested = (l: any) => l.category === 'personnel-salary';
const monthsOver = (l: any) => monthsTested(l) && effectiveQty(l, lines) > contractMonths;
const monthsText = (l: any) =>
  `${countOf(effectiveQty(l, lines), 'months')} of the ${contractMonths}-month contract period`;
const monthsErrText = (l: any) =>
  `${countOf(effectiveQty(l, lines), 'months')} is longer than the ${contractMonths}-month contract period (${contract.periodStart}–${contract.periodEnd}).`;

/* ── Line identity: which control NAMES this line ────────────────────────────
   Four categories know their own line's name better than the vendor does, so
   they replace the free-text description with the thing that actually decides
   it. Every row ships all the variants and reveals one, which is what keeps a
   cloned row correct when it is added to a different category. */
const identOf = (l: any): string =>
  l.category === 'personnel-salary' ? 'position'
  : l.category === 'personnel-fringe' ? 'derived'
  : l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? 'travel-kind'
  : l.category === 'vehicles' ? 'vehicle-kind'
  : 'label';
/** What the line is CALLED, whether that was typed or resolved. */
const displayLabel = (l: any): string =>
  l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? travelLabel(l)
  : l.category === 'personnel-fringe' ? fringeLabel(l, lines)
  : l.category === 'personnel-salary' ? (positionTitle(l.staffKey) || l.label || '')
  : l.label ?? '';
/** The quiet second line a kind picker owes the vendor — its rule, in words. */
const kindNote = (l: any): string =>
  l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? (travelKind(l.travelKind)?.note ?? '')
  : l.category === 'vehicles' ? (vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind)?.note ?? '')
  : l.detail ?? '';

/* ── Vehicles: the kind locks the unit, so the unit stops being an input ── */
const unitLocked = (l: any) => l.category === 'vehicles' && !!vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind);
const lockedUnit = (l: any) => vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind)?.unit ?? l.qtyUnit ?? '';
const lockedUnit2 = (l: any) => vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind)?.secondUnit ?? l.qty2Unit ?? '';

/* ── Work-element allocation ── */
const allocEntries = (l: any): [string, number][] =>
  (Object.entries(l.allocations ?? {}) as [string, number][]).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const allocSum = (l: any) => allocEntries(l).reduce((a, [, p]) => a + p, 0);
const allocSummary = (l: any) => {
  const e = allocEntries(l);
  if (!e.length) return 'Not allocated';
  return e.length === 1 ? `${e[0][0]} · ${e[0][1]}%` : `${e[0][0]} · ${e[0][1]}% +${e.length - 1}`;
};
const allocTitle = (l: any) => {
  const e = allocEntries(l);
  return e.length ? e.map(([id, p]) => `${weLabel(id)} — ${p}%`).join('\n') : 'No work element assigned';
};
const allocErrText = (l: any) => {
  const s = allocSum(l);
  return s === 0
    ? 'No work element assigned — allocations must total 100%.'
    : `Allocated ${s}% — work-element allocations must total 100%.`;
};

/* ── Read-only mirrors, for the COR lens ────────────────────────────────────
   A reviewer does not type into someone else's budget, so in the COR lens every
   entry control is replaced by the VALUE it holds. Rendered as facts, not as
   disabled inputs: a grayed-out field reads as "you failed to fill this in",
   which is a lie about a budget that has already been submitted. Both renderings
   ship in the markup and the role attribute reveals one, so switching lenses
   never loses what the vendor typed. */
const qtyFactText = (l: any) => {
  if (l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL) return travelChain(l);
  const one = `${qtyFmt(effectiveQty(l, lines))} ${l.qtyUnit ?? ''}`.trim();
  return l.qty2 != null ? `${one} × ${qtyFmt(l.qty2)} ${l.qty2Unit ?? ''}`.trim() : one;
};
const allocFactText = (l: any) => {
  const e = allocEntries(l);
  return e.length ? e.map(([id, p]) => `${id} ${p}%`).join(' · ') : 'Not allocated';
};

/* Row state carried as an inline background tint — never a colored left border. */
const rowFlag = (l: any) =>
  gsaOver(l) ? 'gsa'
  : allocSum(l) !== 100 ? 'alloc'
  : !capPass(l) ? 'capital'
  : monthsOver(l) ? 'months'
  : null;

/* ── Grouping: a flat item list per category so the ROW markup is written once,
   whether or not the category is split into sub-blocks. ── */
const linesIn = (catKey: string, blockKey?: string) =>
  lines.filter((l) => l.category === catKey && (blockKey ? l.block === blockKey : true));
const itemsFor = (cat: any): any[] =>
  cat.blocks
    ? cat.blocks.flatMap((b: any) => [
        { kind: 'block', block: b },
        ...linesIn(cat.key, b.key).map((line: any) => ({ kind: 'line', line })),
      ])
    : linesIn(cat.key).map((line: any) => ({ kind: 'line', line }));

/* ── Indirect scope, spelled out so re-scoping is legible in the totals block ── */
const excludedLabels = (cfg: any) =>
  categories.filter((c: any) => !cfg.appliesTo.includes(c.key)).map((c: any) => c.label);
const scopeText = (cfg: any) => {
  const out = excludedLabels(cfg);
  return out.length
    ? `${cfg.appliesTo.length} of ${categories.length} categories in scope — ${out.join(' and ')} excluded`
    : `All ${categories.length} categories in scope`;
};

const t0 = priced.totals;
---

<section class="cbf-lct stack" data-gap="md" data-lct>
  {heading && (
    <header class="stack" data-gap="3xs">
      <h2 class="type-section-title">{heading}</h2>
      {lede && <p class="cbf-lct__lede">{lede}</p>}
    </header>
  )}

  <!-- overflow stays visible at desktop on purpose: the allocation esa-popover,
       the esa-select panels and the add-line esa-dropdown-menu all position
       absolutely and cannot escape a clipping ancestor (same finding as
       cbf-lib-pi-documents). Narrow viewports fall back to horizontal scrolling
       and accept the clipping. -->
  <div class="cbf-lct__scroll">
    <!-- There is deliberately NO global <thead>. One header for nine categories
         forced every section to answer to the same three words, and "Quantity"
         means months here, nights there, and nothing at all in Fringe. Each
         category captions its own columns instead — see `columns` in
         src/data/lib-entry.mjs. The three columns that DON'T move (Amount, Work
         element, the row action) keep one name throughout, because the money
         column has to line up with the subtotals and the table foot. -->
    <table class="cbf-lct__table" aria-label="Line item budget cost lines">
      {categories.map((cat: any) => (
        <tbody data-cat={cat.key}>
          {/* ── Category header: label, note, subtotal, and the inline add ── */}
          <tr class="cbf-lct__cathead">
            <th scope="colgroup" colspan="3">
              <span class="stack" data-gap="3xs">
                <span class="cbf-lct__cat-label">{cat.label}</span>
                {cat.note && <span class="cbf-lct__note">{cat.note}</span>}
              </span>
            </th>
            <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num">
              <strong class="cbf-lct__money" data-cat-total={cat.key}>{usd(priced.byCategory[cat.key] ?? 0)}</strong>
            </td>
            <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2">
              {cat.key === 'travel' ? (
                /* Travel adds TRIPS, not lines — a line without a trip has no
                   locality, no travelers and no occurrence count to stand on. */
                <EsaButton size="sm" color="primary" appearance="outline" icon="plus" data-add-trip>
                  Add trip
                </EsaButton>
              ) : cat.shapes.length > 1 || cat.blocks ? (
                /* More than one legal kind (or more than one sub-block): the add
                   affordance has to let the vendor pick which one, so it is a menu. */
                <esa-dropdown-menu position="below-end" data-add-menu={cat.key}>
                  <EsaButton size="sm" color="primary" appearance="outline" icon="plus">Add line</EsaButton>
                </esa-dropdown-menu>
              ) : (
                <EsaButton
                  size="sm"
                  color="primary"
                  appearance="outline"
                  icon="plus"
                  data-add-line={`${cat.key}||${cat.shapes[0]}`}
                >Add line</EsaButton>
              )}
            </td>
          </tr>

          {/* ── This category's OWN column captions ── */}
          <tr class="cbf-lct__colhead">
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-line">{cat.columns.line}</th>
            {cat.columns.span ? (
              <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-span" colspan="2">{cat.columns.span}</th>
            ) : (
              <>
                <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-qty">
                  {cat.columns.qty ?? <span class="cbf-lct__sr">This section has no quantity column</span>}
                </th>
                <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-rate">{cat.columns.rate}</th>
              </>
            )}
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-num">Amount</th>
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-we">Work element</th>
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-act"><span class="cbf-lct__sr">Row actions</span></th>
          </tr>

          {itemsFor(cat).map((item: any) =>
            item.kind === 'block' ? (
              /* ── Sub-block header with its OWN subtotal ────────────────────
                 Supplies uses this for Office/Field. Travel uses the SAME
                 machinery for trips, and a trip head carries more: it owns the
                 facts every line under it would otherwise repeat — locality,
                 purpose, window, travelers — plus `occurrences`, the multiplier
                 that turns "a quarterly meeting" into one trip instead of four
                 unrelated rows. Raise it and every line below moves together. ── */
              <tr
                class="cbf-lct__blockhead"
                data-blockhead={item.block.key}
                data-trip={item.block.trip ? item.block.key : undefined}
              >
                <th scope="rowgroup" colspan="3">
                  <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                    <span class="cbf-lct__block-label">{item.block.label}</span>

                    {item.block.trip && (
                      <>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-trip-cell="window">
                          {item.block.trip.window}
                        </span>
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="sm" data-trip-controls>
                          {/* Destination is TYPED, not picked. The GSA schedule
                              prices a few hundred localities separately and covers
                              every other place with one standard CONUS row — so a
                              list of listed localities would tell a vendor they may
                              only travel where GSA itemises, which is the opposite
                              of how per diem works. A weir on Rock Creek is a real
                              destination that simply prices at CONUS. */}
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--dest"
                            size="sm"
                            label="Destination"
                            data-trip-field="locality"
                            value={item.block.trip.locality}
                            placeholder="City, ST"
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--purpose"
                            size="sm"
                            label="Purpose"
                            data-trip-field="purpose"
                            value={item.block.trip.purpose}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--trip"
                            size="sm"
                            label="Times it happens"
                            data-trip-field="occurrences"
                            value={String(item.block.trip.occurrences)}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                        </span>
                        {/* What the dropdown used to hide: whether the place typed
                            is separately priced, and therefore what the lines
                            below will actually cost. */}
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-trip-cell="locality-note">
                          {localityNote(item.block.trip)}
                        </span>
                        {/* COR lens: the trip as facts, not as an editor. */}
                        <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-trip-fact>
                          {countOf(item.block.trip.occurrences, 'trips')}
                        </span>
                      </>
                    )}
                  </span>
                </th>
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num">
                  <span class="cbf-lct__money" data-block-total={`${cat.key}:${item.block.key}`}>
                    {usd(priced.byBlock[`${cat.key}:${item.block.key}`] ?? 0)}
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2">
                  {item.block.trip && (
                    /* Adding INSIDE a trip: the menu is the travel kinds, because
                       the kind is what the vendor is actually choosing. */
                    <esa-dropdown-menu position="below-end" data-add-travel={item.block.key}>
                      <EsaButton size="sm" color="secondary" appearance="soft" icon="plus">
                        Add travel line
                      </EsaButton>
                    </esa-dropdown-menu>
                  )}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ) : (
              <tr
                data-line={item.line.id}
                data-shape={item.line.shape}
                data-cat={cat.key}
                data-block={item.line.block ?? ''}
                data-flag={rowFlag(item.line)}
              >
                {/* ── 1. The line's IDENTITY — and the mark, if it carries one ──
                     Four categories know what a line is called better than the
                     vendor does, so they replace free text with the thing that
                     actually decides the name: a position off the roster, a
                     travel kind, a vehicle kind, or — for fringe — the salary
                     line it follows. All five variants ship on every row and one
                     is revealed, which is what keeps a CLONED row correct when it
                     is added to, or moved into, a different category. */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--line">
                  <div class="stack" data-gap="3xs">
                    <span data-ident="label" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'label'}>
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--label"
                        size="md"
                        data-field="label"
                        value={item.line.label}
                        placeholder="Describe this cost line"
                        aria-label={`Cost line description — ${item.line.label || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                    </span>

                    {/* Positions, never names — so it is a roster, not a textbox. */}
                    <span data-ident="position" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'position'}>
                      <esa-select
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        searchable
                        data-position-select
                        placeholder={displayLabel(item.line) || 'Pick a position'}
                        aria-label={`Position — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-select>
                    </span>

                    {/* The travel kind decides the units AND the schedule scope. */}
                    <span data-ident="travel-kind" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'travel-kind'}>
                      <esa-select
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        data-travel-kind-select
                        placeholder={travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.label ?? 'Pick a travel kind'}
                        aria-label={`Travel kind — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-select>
                    </span>

                    {/* The vehicle kind decides the shape: schedule-rated or typed. */}
                    <span data-ident="vehicle-kind" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'vehicle-kind'}>
                      <esa-select
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        data-vehicle-kind-select
                        placeholder={vehicleKind(item.line.vehicleKind)?.label ?? 'Pick a vehicle kind'}
                        aria-label={`Vehicle kind — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-select>
                    </span>

                    {/* Fringe names itself after the position it is a percentage
                        of — there is nothing here for a vendor to decide. */}
                    <span
                      class="cbf-lct__derived"
                      data-ident="derived"
                      data-cell="ident-derived"
                      hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'derived'}
                    >{displayLabel(item.line)}</span>

                    <span class="cbf-lct__fact cbf-lct__fact--label" data-fact="label">{displayLabel(item.line)}</span>
                    <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="detail" hidden={!kindNote(item.line)}>
                      {kindNote(item.line)}
                    </span>

                    {/* The COR's mark, on the line it disputes. Hidden from the
                        vendor until the review is issued — an unfinished review
                        read as a work list is worse than no review. */}
                    <span
                      class="cbf-lct__mark stack"
                      data-gap="3xs"
                      data-mark
                      data-sev={flagOf(item.line)?.severity ?? ''}
                      hidden={!flagOf(item.line) || !reviewIssued}
                    >
                      <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                        <span data-mark-sev={SEVERITIES.REQUIRED}>
                          <EsaBadge
                            value={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.REQUIRED].short}
                            variant={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.REQUIRED].variant}
                            size="sm"
                          />
                        </span>
                        <span data-mark-sev={SEVERITIES.QUESTION}>
                          <EsaBadge
                            value={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.QUESTION].short}
                            variant={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.QUESTION].variant}
                            size="sm"
                          />
                        </span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__mark-reason" data-mark-reason>
                          {flagOf(item.line) ? reasonLabel(flagOf(item.line)!.reason) : ''}
                        </span>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__mark-note" data-mark-note>
                        {flagOf(item.line)?.note ?? ''}
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-mark-by>
                        {flagOf(item.line) ? `${flagOf(item.line)!.author} · ${flagOf(item.line)!.date}` : ''}
                      </span>
                    </span>
                  </div>
                </td>

                {/* ── 2. Quantity — collapsed away entirely for shapes C and D ──
                     There is no "rate source" column: what governs a rate lives in
                     the Rate cell, beside the number it produces. */}
                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.LUMP ? (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--void" colspan="2">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note">No quantity, no rate — one amount.</span>
                      {item.line.sheet && (
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                          {/* The seam: a lump HERE, a full recursed category set THERE. */}
                          <EsaButton
                            size="sm"
                            color="secondary"
                            appearance="soft"
                            icon="external-link"
                            data-open-sheet={item.line.sheet}
                            data-open-sheet-line={item.line.id}
                          >Open subcontract sheet</EsaButton>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note">Itemized on its own sheet — no indirect applied there.</span>
                        </span>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </td>
                ) : item.line.shape === SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE ? (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--void">
                    <span class="cbf-lct__void-mark">No quantity</span>
                  </td>
                ) : item.line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? (
                  /* ── Travel quantity: occurrences × count × duration ─────────
                     Occurrences is the only one RESOLVED rather than typed, and
                     it is never per-line: a trip that happens six times makes
                     every cost in it happen six times, and a cost that happens a
                     different number of times is a different trip.

                     The count and the duration are this line's own. An earlier
                     pass held the count on the trip as "travelers" and gave every
                     row an override button — but a lodging line counts ROOMS, a
                     mileage line counts VEHICLES, and per diem counts TRAVELERS.
                     Those are three different numbers, so one shared field could
                     not be right for all of them, and the button next to it was
                     the design admitting so. A new line seeds from the last line
                     in the trip counting the SAME unit instead. Both units are
                     fixed by the kind rather than spelled by the vendor. */
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--qty">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <span class="cbf-lct__derived" data-cell="travel-chain">
                        {travelChain(item.line)}
                      </span>

                      <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-travel-count>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                          size="md"
                          data-field="count"
                          value={numText(travelCount(item.line))}
                          aria-label={`Count — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-cell="travel-count-unit">
                          {travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.countLabel ?? ''}
                        </span>
                      </span>

                      {/* Duration — always typed, unit always fixed by the kind. */}
                      <span
                        class="cluster"
                        data-gap="2xs"
                        data-travel-duration
                        hidden={!travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.durationLabel}
                      >
                        <span class="cbf-lct__times" aria-hidden="true">×</span>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                          size="md"
                          data-field="duration"
                          value={numText(item.line.duration)}
                          aria-label={`Duration — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-cell="travel-duration-unit">
                          {travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.durationLabel ?? ''}
                        </span>
                      </span>
                      {/* No separate COR mirror here: the resolved chain above IS
                          the value, and it is shown to both roles. */}
                    </div>
                  </td>
                ) : (
                  <>
                    {/* ── 3. Quantity (typed, or DERIVED when it follows a line) ── */}
                    <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--qty">
                      <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-qty-typed hidden={!!item.line.qtyFrom}>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                            size="md"
                            data-field="qty"
                            value={numText(item.line.qty)}
                            aria-label={`Quantity — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          {/* The unit is only a question where the category leaves
                              it open. A vehicle kind already fixed it to months,
                              so it reads as a value — not as an empty box the
                              vendor has to guess the house word for. */}
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--unit"
                            size="md"
                            data-field="qtyUnit"
                            data-unit-open
                            value={item.line.qtyUnit ?? ''}
                            placeholder="unit"
                            hidden={unitLocked(item.line)}
                            aria-label={`Unit — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-unit-locked hidden={!unitLocked(item.line)}>
                            {lockedUnit(item.line)}
                          </span>
                        </span>

                        {/* The contract period is a ceiling on staffing months,
                            and it is arithmetic — so the form does it, in both
                            states, rather than leaving it to the reviewer. */}
                        <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-months-wrap hidden={!monthsTested(item.line)}>
                          <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                            <span data-months="pass" hidden={monthsOver(item.line)}>
                              <EsaBadge value="Within contract period" variant="success" />
                            </span>
                            <span data-months="fail" hidden={!monthsOver(item.line)}>
                              <EsaBadge value="Past contract period" variant="danger" />
                            </span>
                          </span>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-months-note>{monthsText(item.line)}</span>
                          <span data-months-err hidden={!monthsOver(item.line)}>
                            <EsaFieldError message={monthsErrText(item.line)} icon />
                          </span>
                        </span>

                        {/* Read-only, and it names the line it follows. */}
                        <span class="stack" data-gap="3xs" data-qty-derived hidden={!item.line.qtyFrom}>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__derived" data-cell="derived-qty">
                            {qtyFmt(effectiveQty(item.line, lines))} {item.line.qtyUnit}
                          </span>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="derived-src">
                            {item.line.qtyFrom ? `follows ${labelOfId(item.line.qtyFrom)}` : ''}
                          </span>
                        </span>

                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-qty2 hidden={item.line.qty2 == null}>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__times" aria-hidden="true">×</span>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                            size="md"
                            data-field="qty2"
                            value={numText(item.line.qty2)}
                            aria-label={`Second quantity — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--unit"
                            size="md"
                            data-field="qty2Unit"
                            data-unit2-open
                            value={item.line.qty2Unit ?? ''}
                            placeholder="unit"
                            hidden={unitLocked(item.line)}
                            aria-label={`Second unit — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-unit2-locked hidden={!unitLocked(item.line)}>
                            {lockedUnit2(item.line)}
                          </span>
                        </span>
                        <span data-qty2-add hidden={item.line.qty2 != null}>
                          <EsaButton size="xs" color="secondary" appearance="soft" data-add-qty2>
                            Add a second quantity
                          </EsaButton>
                        </span>

                        {/* COR lens: the quantity as a value. A derived quantity
                            already reads as one, so it keeps its own cell. */}
                        <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="qty" hidden={!!item.line.qtyFrom}>
                          {qtyFactText(item.line)}
                        </span>
                      </div>
                    </td>
                  </>
                )}

                {/* ── 4. Rate — typed (A), referenced (B), a percentage (C) ── */}
                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.QTY_RATE && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--rate"
                        size="md"
                        prefix="$"
                        data-field="rate"
                        value={numText(item.line.rate)}
                        aria-label={`Cost per unit — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="rate">{money(item.line.rate ?? 0)}</span>

                      {/* The capitalization threshold, tested in BOTH directions
                          and sitting with the per-item cost it is a test OF. A
                          $6,200 trap assembly filed under Supplies is the same
                          error as a $400 toner cartridge filed under Capital —
                          so the failing row offers the move rather than only
                          naming the problem. */}
                      <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-cap-wrap hidden={!thresholdTested(item.line)}>
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                          <span data-cap="pass" hidden={!capPass(item.line)}>
                            <EsaBadge value={capPassText(item.line)} variant="success" />
                          </span>
                          <span data-cap="fail" hidden={capPass(item.line)}>
                            <EsaBadge value={capFailText(item.line)} variant="danger" />
                          </span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cap-note>{capText(item.line)}</span>
                        <span data-cap-err hidden={capPass(item.line)}>
                          <EsaFieldError message={capErrText(item.line)} icon />
                        </span>
                        <span data-cap-move hidden={capPass(item.line)}>
                          <EsaButton size="xs" color="secondary" appearance="soft" icon="arrow-right">
                            {capMoveText(item.line)}
                          </EsaButton>
                        </span>
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {/* ── Travel's rate cell carries BOTH sources ─────────────────
                     Lodging, per diem and mileage are referenced from the GSA
                     schedule; airfare, ground transport and registration are
                     typed. Because a vendor can switch a line's kind in place,
                     both renderings ship in the one cell and the kind reveals
                     one — the same device the two role lenses already use. */}
                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      {/* Referenced — and the combobox is filtered by the KIND, so
                          a lodging line cannot reach an M&IE row and a mileage
                          line reaches the fleet rows it actually needs. */}
                      <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-travel-rate="gsa" hidden={!isScheduleRated(item.line)}>
                        <esa-combobox
                          class="cbf-lct__select"
                          size="md"
                          data-gsa-select
                          placeholder={gsaLabel(item.line)}
                          search-placeholder="Search locality…"
                          aria-label={`GSA schedule row — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-combobox>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__ref" data-cell="ref-rate">{money(effectiveRate(item.line))}</span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="ref-src">{gsaSrcText(item.line)}</span>

                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                          <span data-gsa="pass" hidden={overridden(item.line)}>
                            <EsaBadge value="Matches GSA schedule" variant="success" />
                          </span>
                          <span data-gsa="under" hidden={!overridden(item.line) || gsaOver(item.line)}>
                            <EsaBadge value="Overridden, within ceiling" variant="warning" />
                          </span>
                          <span data-gsa="over" hidden={!gsaOver(item.line)}>
                            <EsaBadge value="Over GSA ceiling" variant="danger" />
                          </span>
                        </span>

                        <span data-override-wrap hidden={!overridden(item.line)}>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--rate"
                            size="md"
                            prefix="$"
                            data-field="rateOverride"
                            value={numText(item.line.rateOverride)}
                            aria-label={`Overridden rate — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                        </span>
                        <span>
                          <EsaButton size="xs" color="secondary" appearance="soft" data-toggle-override>
                            {overridden(item.line) ? 'Use schedule rate' : 'Override rate'}
                          </EsaButton>
                        </span>
                        <span data-gsa-err hidden={!gsaOver(item.line)}>
                          <EsaFieldError message={gsaErrText(item.line)} icon />
                        </span>
                      </span>

                      {/* Typed — no schedule governs an airfare quote or a parking
                          estimate, so the cell says so rather than implying one. */}
                      <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-travel-rate="typed" hidden={isScheduleRated(item.line)}>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--rate"
                          size="md"
                          prefix="$"
                          data-field="rate"
                          value={numText(item.line.rate)}
                          aria-label={`Rate — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note">No schedule governs this rate — quote it.</span>
                      </span>

                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="travel-rate">{money(effectiveRate(item.line))}</span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      {/* Which schedule row the rate is drawn FROM sits with the
                          rate itself — the authority and the number it produces
                          belong in one cell, not two columns apart. */}
                      {/* A combobox, not a select: the real schedule is thousands
                          of localities, so the row has to be SEARCHED for. That is
                          also the honest argument for "referenced, not typed". */}
                      <esa-combobox
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        data-gsa-select
                        placeholder={gsaLabel(item.line)}
                        search-placeholder="Search locality or rate type…"
                        aria-label={`GSA schedule row — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-combobox>
                      {/* Referenced, not typed: the schedule value plus its source. */}
                      <span class="cbf-lct__ref" data-cell="ref-rate">{money(effectiveRate(item.line))}</span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="ref-src">{gsaSrcText(item.line)}</span>

                      <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                        <span data-gsa="pass" hidden={overridden(item.line)}>
                          <EsaBadge value="Matches GSA schedule" variant="success" />
                        </span>
                        <span data-gsa="under" hidden={!overridden(item.line) || gsaOver(item.line)}>
                          <EsaBadge value="Overridden, within ceiling" variant="warning" />
                        </span>
                        <span data-gsa="over" hidden={!gsaOver(item.line)}>
                          <EsaBadge value="Over GSA ceiling" variant="danger" />
                        </span>
                      </span>

                      <span data-override-wrap hidden={!overridden(item.line)}>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--rate"
                          size="md"
                          prefix="$"
                          data-field="rateOverride"
                          value={numText(item.line.rateOverride)}
                          aria-label={`Overridden rate — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                      </span>
                      <span>
                        <EsaButton size="xs" color="secondary" appearance="soft" data-toggle-override>
                          {overridden(item.line) ? 'Use schedule rate' : 'Override rate'}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>
                      <span data-gsa-err hidden={!gsaOver(item.line)}>
                        <EsaFieldError message={gsaErrText(item.line)} icon />
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--pct"
                        size="md"
                        suffix="%"
                        data-field="pct"
                        value={((item.line.pct ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(1)}
                        aria-label={`Percentage of base — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="pct">{((item.line.pct ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(1)}%</span>

                      {/* A percentage is meaningless without its base, so the base
                          picker and the resolved dollars ride with the percentage. */}
                      <esa-select
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        data-base-select
                        placeholder={baseLabelFor(item.line, lines)}
                        aria-label={`Percentage base — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-select>
                      {/* The dependency, made visible on the row itself. */}
                      <span class="cbf-lct__derived" data-cell="base">
                        {money(baseAmountFor(item.line, lines, priced.amounts))} base ·
                        {' '}{baseLabelFor(item.line, lines)}
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {/* ── 5. Amount — typed only for a lump; derived everywhere else ── */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num">
                  {item.line.shape === SHAPES.LUMP ? (
                    <>
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--amount"
                        size="md"
                        prefix="$"
                        data-field="amount"
                        value={numText(item.line.amount)}
                        aria-label={`Amount — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                      <strong class="cbf-lct__fact cbf-lct__money" data-fact="amount">
                        {usd(amt(item.line.id))}
                      </strong>
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <strong class="cbf-lct__money" data-cell="amount">{usd(amt(item.line.id))}</strong>
                  )}
                </td>

                {/* ── 6. Work-element allocation — on EVERY row, whatever the shape ── */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--we">
                  <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                    <esa-popover position="bottom" trigger="click" offset="6">
                      <span data-alloc-state="set" hidden={allocSum(item.line) !== 100}>
                        <EsaButton size="md" color="secondary" appearance="outline" title={allocTitle(item.line)}>
                          {allocSummary(item.line)}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>
                      <span data-alloc-state="unset" hidden={allocSum(item.line) === 100}>
                        <EsaButton size="md" color="warning" appearance="outline" title={allocTitle(item.line)}>
                          {allocSummary(item.line)}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>

                      <div slot="content" class="cbf-lct__alloc stack" data-gap="sm" data-alloc-panel>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__alloc-head">Work-element allocation</span>
                        {workElements.map((we: any) => (
                          <div class="cbf-lct__alloc-row">
                            <span class="cbf-lct__alloc-label">{weLabel(we.id)}</span>
                            <esa-text-field
                              class="cbf-lct__alloc-input"
                              size="md"
                              suffix="%"
                              data-alloc-we={we.id}
                              value={item.line.allocations?.[we.id] != null ? String(item.line.allocations[we.id]) : ''}
                              aria-label={`${weLabel(we.id)} percent — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                            ></esa-text-field>
                          </div>
                        ))}
                        <p class="cbf-lct__alloc-sum">
                          <span>Allocated</span>
                          <strong data-alloc-sum>{allocSum(item.line)}%</strong>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note">of 100%</span>
                        </p>
                        <span data-alloc-panel-err hidden={allocSum(item.line) === 100}>
                          <EsaFieldError message={allocErrText(item.line)} icon />
                        </span>
                      </div>
                    </esa-popover>

                    {/* COR lens: the split as a value, not an editor. */}
                    <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="alloc" title={allocTitle(item.line)}>
                      {allocFactText(item.line)}
                    </span>

                    <span data-alloc-row-err hidden={allocSum(item.line) === 100}>
                      <EsaFieldError message={allocErrText(item.line)} icon />
                    </span>
                  </div>
                </td>

                {/* ── 7. Row action — delete for the vendor, MARK for the COR ──
                    The same slot, because they are the same thing from each side:
                    the one operation that role performs on a line it is looking
                    at. The mark editor opens on the row so the note is written
                    with the numbers it is about still on screen. ── */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--act">
                  <span data-delete-line>
                    <EsaIconButton icon="trash-2" size="sm" label={`Delete line — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`} />
                  </span>

                  <span data-mark-cell>
                    <esa-popover position="left" trigger="click" offset="6" data-mark-popover>
                      <span data-mark-state="off" hidden={!!flagOf(item.line)}>
                        <EsaIconButton
                          icon="pencil"
                          size="sm"
                          label={`Mark this line for the vendor — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        />
                      </span>
                      <span data-mark-state="on" hidden={!flagOf(item.line)}>
                        <EsaIconButton
                          icon="triangle-alert"
                          size="sm"
                          label={`Edit the mark on this line — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        />
                      </span>

                      <div slot="content" class="cbf-lct__markform stack" data-gap="sm" data-mark-panel>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__alloc-head">Mark this line for the vendor</span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-mark-form-line>{displayLabel(item.line)}</span>
                        <esa-radio-group data-mark-sev-input size="md" label="How hard is this?"></esa-radio-group>
                        <esa-select
                          data-mark-reason-input
                          size="md"
                          label="Reason"
                          placeholder="Pick a reason"
                        ></esa-select>
                        <esa-textarea
                          data-mark-note-input
                          label="What has to change"
                          rows="3"
                          auto-resize
                          help-text="Written to the vendor. Say what to do, not just what is wrong."
                          value={flagOf(item.line)?.note ?? ''}
                        ></esa-textarea>
                        <span class="repel" data-gap="sm">
                          <span data-mark-remove hidden={!flagOf(item.line)}>
                            <EsaButton size="sm" color="ghost">Remove mark</EsaButton>
                          </span>
                          <EsaButton size="sm" color="primary" data-mark-save>Save mark</EsaButton>
                        </span>
                      </div>
                    </esa-popover>
                  </span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ),
          )}
        </tbody>
      ))}

      {/* ── The close: direct, indirect base, indirect, and the ceiling test ── */}
      <tfoot class="cbf-lct__totals">
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" colspan="3">
            Direct cost
            <span class="cbf-lct__note">Every cost line above, priced in dependency order.</span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><span data-total="direct">{usd(t0.direct)}</span></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr data-indirect-row>
          <th scope="row" colspan="3">
            Indirect base
            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-indirect-scope>{scopeText(indirectConfig)}</span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><span data-total="indirectBase">{usd(t0.indirectBase)}</span></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr data-indirect-row>
          <th scope="row" colspan="3">
            Indirect at <span data-indirect-rate>{pct(indirectConfig.rate)}</span>
            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-indirect-ref>{indirectConfig.referenceDoc}</span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><span data-total="indirect">{usd(t0.indirect)}</span></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="cbf-lct__grand" data-indirect-row>
          <th scope="row" colspan="3">
            Total this contract period
            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-total-note>
              {pct(t0.pctOfValue, 1)} of the {usd(contract.contractValue)} Contract Value ·
              {' '}{usd(t0.remaining)} remaining
            </span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><strong data-total="total">{usd(t0.total)}</strong></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
      </tfoot>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-text-field';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-select';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-combobox';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-popover';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-dropdown-menu';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-radio-group';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-textarea';
  import {
    SEVERITIES,
    REASONS,
    severityMeta,
    reasonLabel,
    reviewMeta,
    loadReview,
    saveReview,
    flagFor,
    isIssued,
    fireReview,
  } from '../../data/lib-review.mjs';
  import {
    SHAPES,
    categories,
    costLines,
    priceLines,
    effectiveRate,
    effectiveQty,
    baseAmountFor,
    baseLabelFor,
    dependentsOf,
    gsaRates,
    gsaRatesFor,
    gsaRatesForTravelKind,
    gsaRatesForVehicleKind,
    gsaRate,
    isScheduleRated,
    weLabel,
    unallocatedLines,
    gsaFailingLines,
    thresholdMisfiled,
    monthsOverPeriod,
    perItemCost,
    contract,
    contractMonths,
    positions,
    positionTitle,
    trips,
    tripOf,
    tripLabel,
    TRAVEL_KINDS,
    travelKind,
    travelChain,
    travelCount,
    seedCountFor,
    localityNote,
    gsaRowFor,
    travelLabel,
    fringeLabel,
    VEHICLE_KINDS,
    vehicleKind,
    countOf,
    indirectConfig,
    usd,
    pct,
    qtyFmt,
  } from '../../data/lib-entry.mjs';

  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-lct]');
  if (root) init(root);

  function init(root: HTMLElement) {
    /* Working copies — edits, added and deleted lines mutate THESE, never the
       module's exports, so every derived number stays honest across a repaint. */
    const lines: any[] = costLines.map((l: any) => ({ ...l, allocations: { ...(l.allocations ?? {}) } }));
    let cfg: any = { ...indirectConfig, appliesTo: [...indirectConfig.appliesTo] };
    let seq = 0;

    const $ = <T extends HTMLElement = HTMLElement>(sel: string, scope: ParentNode = root) =>
      scope.querySelector<T>(sel);
    const $$ = (sel: string, scope: ParentNode = root) =>
      Array.from(scope.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(sel));
    const rowOf = (id: string) => $(`tr[data-line="${id}"]`);
    const lineById = (id: string) => lines.find((l) => l.id === id);
    const num = (s: string) => {
      const v = parseFloat(String(s ?? '').replace(/[^0-9.\-]/g, ''));
      return Number.isFinite(v) ? v : 0;
    };

    /* ── Text helpers — mirrored from the frontmatter so SSR and repaint agree.
       (The shape descriptor itself is never repainted: row templates are captured
       PER SHAPE, so a cloned row already carries the right one.) ── */
    const cents = (n: number) => Math.abs((n ?? 0) % 1) > 0.0001;
    const money = (n: number) => usd(n, { cents: cents(n) });
    const numText = (n: number | null | undefined) =>
      n == null ? '' : cents(n) ? String(n) : n.toLocaleString('en-US');
    const labelOfId = (id: string) => lines.find((l) => l.id === id)?.label ?? id;

    const gsaOf = (l: any) => gsaRate(l.gsaKey);
    /* Any SCHEDULE-RATED line can be overridden — shape B, and the GSA-rated travel
   kinds. Keying this to shape B alone left an overridden travel line showing
   "Matches GSA schedule" and "Over GSA ceiling" at the same time. */
const overridden = (l: any) => isScheduleRated(l) && l.rateOverride != null;
    const gsaOver = (l: any) => {
      const g = gsaOf(l);
      return !!(g && l.rateOverride != null && l.rateOverride > g.ceiling);
    };
    const gsaSrcText = (l: any) => {
      const g = gsaOf(l);
      /* The dropdown above already NAMES the schedule row (location · kind · season).
     Restating it here said the same string twice and explained nothing, so this
     line says what the control DOES instead: the rate is referenced, not typed,
     and the schedule sets the ceiling the line is checked against. */
  return g
    ? `Rate referenced from this GSA row — not typed. Ceiling ${money(g.ceiling)}.`
    : 'Pick the GSA schedule row this line references — it sets the rate.';
    };
    const gsaErrText = (l: any) => {
      const g = gsaOf(l);
      if (!g) return 'Select the GSA schedule row this line references.';
      return `${money(l.rateOverride)} is over the ${g.kind} ceiling of ${money(g.ceiling)} for ${g.location} (${g.season}) — ${money(l.rateOverride - g.ceiling)} over.`;
    };

    /* ── The capitalization threshold, tested in BOTH directions ── */
    const onThreshold = (l: any) => l.category === 'capital-equipment' || l.category === 'supplies';
    const thresholdTested = (l: any) => onThreshold(l) && l.shape === SHAPES.QTY_RATE;
    const isCapital = (l: any) => l.category === 'capital-equipment';
    const perItem = (l: any, a: any) => perItemCost(l, a);
    const capPass = (l: any, a: any) =>
      !thresholdTested(l) ||
      (isCapital(l)
        ? perItem(l, a) >= contract.capitalizationThreshold
        : perItem(l, a) < contract.capitalizationThreshold);
    const capText = (l: any, a: any) =>
      `${money(perItem(l, a))} per item · threshold ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)}`;
    const capErrText = (l: any, a: any) =>
      isCapital(l)
        ? `${money(perItem(l, a))} per item is below the ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} capitalization threshold — this item belongs in Supplies & Equipment.`
        : `${money(perItem(l, a))} per item is at or above the ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} capitalization threshold — this item belongs in Capital Equipment.`;
    const capPassText = (l: any) =>
      isCapital(l) ? 'Meets capitalization threshold' : 'Below capitalization threshold';
    const capFailText = (l: any) =>
      isCapital(l) ? 'Below capitalization threshold' : 'Over capitalization threshold';
    const capMoveText = (l: any) =>
      isCapital(l) ? 'Move to Supplies & Equipment' : 'Move to Capital Equipment';
    /** Where a misfiled item belongs, and which shape it lands as. */
    const capMoveTarget = (l: any) =>
      isCapital(l)
        ? { category: 'supplies', block: 'field' }
        : { category: 'capital-equipment', block: '' };

    /* ── The contract period as a ceiling on staffing months ── */
    const monthsTested = (l: any) => l.category === 'personnel-salary';
    const monthsOver = (l: any) => monthsTested(l) && effectiveQty(l, lines) > contractMonths;
    const monthsText = (l: any) =>
      `${countOf(effectiveQty(l, lines), 'months')} of the ${contractMonths}-month contract period`;
    const monthsErrText = (l: any) =>
      `${countOf(effectiveQty(l, lines), 'months')} is longer than the ${contractMonths}-month contract period (${contract.periodStart}–${contract.periodEnd}).`;

    /* ── Line identity — mirrored from the frontmatter ── */
    const identOf = (l: any): string =>
      l.category === 'personnel-salary' ? 'position'
      : l.category === 'personnel-fringe' ? 'derived'
      : l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? 'travel-kind'
      : l.category === 'vehicles' ? 'vehicle-kind'
      : 'label';
    const displayLabel = (l: any): string =>
      l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? travelLabel(l)
      : l.category === 'personnel-fringe' ? fringeLabel(l, lines)
      : l.category === 'personnel-salary' ? (positionTitle(l.staffKey) || l.label || '')
      : l.label ?? '';
    const kindNote = (l: any): string =>
      l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? (travelKind(l.travelKind)?.note ?? '')
      : l.category === 'vehicles' ? (vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind)?.note ?? '')
      : l.detail ?? '';

    /* ── Vehicles: the kind locks the unit ── */
    const unitLocked = (l: any) => l.category === 'vehicles' && !!vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind);
    const lockedUnit = (l: any) => vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind)?.unit ?? l.qtyUnit ?? '';
    const lockedUnit2 = (l: any) => vehicleKind(l.vehicleKind)?.secondUnit ?? l.qty2Unit ?? '';

    /* Keep the stored label in step with a derived one, so the review panel — which
       addresses lines BY NAME — never quotes a name the grid stopped showing. */
    const syncLabel = (l: any) => {
      if (l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL || l.category === 'personnel-fringe' || l.category === 'personnel-salary') {
        l.label = displayLabel(l);
      }
    };

    const allocEntries = (l: any): [string, number][] =>
      (Object.entries(l.allocations ?? {}) as [string, number][]).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
    const allocSum = (l: any) => allocEntries(l).reduce((a, [, p]) => a + p, 0);
    const allocSummary = (l: any) => {
      const e = allocEntries(l);
      if (!e.length) return 'Not allocated';
      return e.length === 1 ? `${e[0][0]} · ${e[0][1]}%` : `${e[0][0]} · ${e[0][1]}% +${e.length - 1}`;
    };
    const allocTitle = (l: any) => {
      const e = allocEntries(l);
      return e.length ? e.map(([id, p]) => `${weLabel(id)} — ${p}%`).join('\n') : 'No work element assigned';
    };
    const allocErrText = (l: any) => {
      const s = allocSum(l);
      return s === 0
        ? 'No work element assigned — allocations must total 100%.'
        : `Allocated ${s}% — work-element allocations must total 100%.`;
    };

    /* The COR lens's read-only mirrors — mirrored from the frontmatter so a
       server render and a client repaint print the same value. */
    const qtyFactText = (l: any) => {
      const one = `${qtyFmt(effectiveQty(l, lines))} ${l.qtyUnit ?? ''}`.trim();
      return l.qty2 != null ? `${one} × ${qtyFmt(l.qty2)} ${l.qty2Unit ?? ''}`.trim() : one;
    };
    const allocFactText = (l: any) => {
      const e = allocEntries(l);
      return e.length ? e.map(([id, p]) => `${id} ${p}%`).join(' · ') : 'Not allocated';
    };

    const excludedLabels = (c: any) =>
      categories.filter((k: any) => !c.appliesTo.includes(k.key)).map((k: any) => k.label);
    const scopeText = (c: any) => {
      const out = excludedLabels(c);
      return out.length
        ? `${c.appliesTo.length} of ${categories.length} categories in scope — ${out.join(' and ')} excluded`
        : `All ${categories.length} categories in scope`;
    };

    /* ── Tiny DOM setters (esa-* internals are stable markup) ── */
    const show = (el: Element | null, on: boolean) => { if (el) (el as HTMLElement).hidden = !on; };
    const setText = (el: Element | null, text: string) => { if (el) el.textContent = text; };
    /** esa-field-error renders <p.esa-field-error><span.esa-field-error__text>. */
    const setErrText = (wrap: Element | null, text: string) =>
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-field-error__text') ?? null, text);
    /** esa-badge renders <span.esa-badge><span.esa-badge__text>. */
    const setBadgeText = (wrap: Element | null, text: string) =>
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-badge__text') ?? null, text);
    /** esa-button renders <span.esa-button><button><span.esa-button__label>. */
    const setBtnLabel = (wrap: Element | null, text: string, title?: string) => {
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-button__label') ?? null, text);
      if (title != null) wrap?.querySelector('button')?.setAttribute('title', title);
    };

    /* ── Select option sets ── */
    /* Scoped per LINE, and now per KIND — which is the tighter, truer filter.
       Scoping by category already stopped a travel line reaching a fleet lease
       row, but it still let a lodging line pick an M&IE rate, which is the error
       that actually happens. The kind narrows the schedule to the rows that kind
       can legally reference, so most lines see a handful of localities instead of
       the whole schedule. */
    const gsaOptionsFor = (line: any) => {
      const rows =
        line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL
          ? gsaRatesForTravelKind(line.travelKind)
          : line.category === 'vehicles' && line.vehicleKind
            ? gsaRatesForVehicleKind(line.vehicleKind)
            : gsaRatesFor(line.category);
      return rows.map((g: any) => ({
        label: `${g.location} · ${g.kind} · ${g.season}`,
        value: g.key,
      }));
    };
    const positionOptions = () => positions.map((p: any) => ({ label: p.title, value: p.key }));
    const travelKindOptions = () => TRAVEL_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({ label: k.label, value: k.key }));
    const vehicleKindOptions = () => VEHICLE_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({ label: k.label, value: k.key }));
    const baseOptions = () => [
      ...lines
        .filter((l) => l.category === 'personnel-salary' && l.staffKey)
        .map((l) => ({ label: `Salary — ${l.label || '(untitled line)'}`, value: `staff:${l.staffKey}` })),
      ...categories.map((c: any) => ({ label: `Category — ${c.label}`, value: `category:${c.key}` })),
    ];
    const baseValueOf = (l: any) => {
      const b = l.base ?? {};
      if (b.type === 'staff') return `staff:${b.staffKey}`;
      if (b.type === 'category') return `category:${b.category}`;
      return '';
    };
    let baseSig = '';

    /* ── The review layer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The grid owns MARKING (a mark is made on the row it is about); the review
       panel owns the list, the memo and the decision. Both read and write the
       same review object and announce it with fireReview(), so neither ever
       reaches into the other's DOM. */
    let review: any = loadReview();
    const role = () => (document.documentElement.dataset.cbfRole === 'cor' ? 'cor' : 'vendor');
    const markOf = (id: string) => flagFor(review, id);

    const SEV_OPTIONS = [
      { label: severityMeta[SEVERITIES.REQUIRED].label, value: SEVERITIES.REQUIRED },
      { label: severityMeta[SEVERITIES.QUESTION].label, value: SEVERITIES.QUESTION },
    ];
    const REASON_OPTIONS = REASONS.map((r: any) => ({ label: r.label, value: r.key }));

    /** Seed one row's mark editor from the mark it currently carries. */
    const fillMarkForm = (tr: HTMLElement, line: any) => {
      const f = markOf(line.id);
      const sev: any = $('[data-mark-sev-input]', tr);
      if (sev) { sev.options = SEV_OPTIONS; sev.value = f?.severity ?? SEVERITIES.REQUIRED; }
      const reason: any = $('[data-mark-reason-input]', tr);
      if (reason) { reason.options = REASON_OPTIONS; reason.value = f?.reason ?? ''; }
      const note: any = $('[data-mark-note-input]', tr);
      if (note) note.value = f?.note ?? '';
      setText($('[data-mark-form-line]', tr), line.label || '(untitled line)');
    };

    /** The mark as the two roles see it: always to the COR, only once issued to
        the vendor. */
    const paintMark = (tr: HTMLElement | null, line: any) => {
      if (!tr) return;
      const f = markOf(line.id);
      const visible = !!f && (role() === 'cor' || isIssued(review));
      const band = $('[data-mark]', tr);
      show(band, visible);
      if (band) band.dataset.sev = f?.severity ?? '';
      if (f) {
        setText($('[data-mark-reason]', tr), reasonLabel(f.reason));
        setText($('[data-mark-note]', tr), f.note || 'No note written.');
        setText($('[data-mark-by]', tr), `${f.author} · ${f.date}`);
      }
      show($('[data-mark-state="off"]', tr), !f);
      show($('[data-mark-state="on"]', tr), !!f);
      show($('[data-mark-remove]', tr), !!f);
    };

    const paintReview = () => lines.forEach((l) => paintMark(rowOf(l.id), l));

    /** Write the review, tell everyone, repaint the rows. */
    const commitReview = () => {
      saveReview(review);
      paintReview();
      fireReview(review);
    };

    /* ── Pristine row templates, one per SHAPE, captured BEFORE any edit ────────
       Cloning a real, freshly-rendered row of the same shape is how an added line
       gets its markup: the new row's esa-* legos and its colspan collapse are
       byte-identical to the SSR ones, with zero duplicated markup. */
    const rowTemplates: Record<string, HTMLTableRowElement> = {};
    Object.values(SHAPES).forEach((shape: any) => {
      const src = $$(`tr[data-shape="${shape}"]`)[0] as HTMLTableRowElement | undefined;
      if (src) rowTemplates[shape] = src.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
    });

    /* ── Painting: fillRow writes identity + entry VALUES (never during typing),
       paintRow writes everything DERIVED. ── */
    const fillRow = (tr: HTMLElement, line: any) => {
      tr.dataset.line = line.id;
      tr.dataset.shape = line.shape;
      tr.dataset.cat = line.category;
      tr.dataset.block = line.block ?? '';

      $$('[data-field]', tr).forEach((el: any) => {
        const key = el.dataset.field;
        if (key === 'label') el.value = line.label ?? '';
        else if (key === 'qtyUnit') el.value = line.qtyUnit ?? '';
        else if (key === 'qty2Unit') el.value = line.qty2Unit ?? '';
        else if (key === 'pct') el.value = ((line.pct ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(1);
        else if (key === 'count') el.value = numText(travelCount(line));
        else el.value = numText(line[key]);
      });
      $$('[data-alloc-we]', tr).forEach((el: any) => {
        const p = line.allocations?.[el.dataset.allocWe];
        el.value = p != null ? String(p) : '';
      });

      const gsaSel: any = $('[data-gsa-select]', tr);
      if (gsaSel) { gsaSel.options = gsaOptionsFor(line); gsaSel.value = line.gsaKey ?? ''; }
      const baseSel: any = $('[data-base-select]', tr);
      if (baseSel) { baseSel.options = baseOptions(); baseSel.value = baseValueOf(line); }
      /* The three identity pickers. Options are constant lists, so they are safe
         to seed on every fill — only the revealed one is ever reachable. */
      const posSel: any = $('[data-position-select]', tr);
      if (posSel) { posSel.options = positionOptions(); posSel.value = line.staffKey ?? ''; }
      const tkSel: any = $('[data-travel-kind-select]', tr);
      if (tkSel) { tkSel.options = travelKindOptions(); tkSel.value = line.travelKind ?? ''; }
      const vkSel: any = $('[data-vehicle-kind-select]', tr);
      if (vkSel) { vkSel.options = vehicleKindOptions(); vkSel.value = line.vehicleKind ?? ''; }
      const del = $('[data-delete-line] button', tr);
      const name = displayLabel(line) || 'new line';
      del?.setAttribute('aria-label', `Delete line — ${name}`);
      del?.setAttribute('title', `Delete line — ${name}`);
    };

    const paintRow = (tr: HTMLElement | null, line: any, priced: any) => {
      if (!tr) return;
      const a = priced.amounts;
      const sum = allocSum(line);
      const over = gsaOver(line);

      /* Basis — shape C shows its resolved base dollars, which is the whole point:
         the dependency is legible ON the row, not implied by it. */
      const baseCell = $('[data-cell="base"]', tr);
      if (baseCell) {
        setText(baseCell, `${money(baseAmountFor(line, lines, a))} base · ${baseLabelFor(line, lines)}`);
      }

      /* Identity — reveal the one control that names a line in THIS category, and
         keep the derived names (fringe, travel) current. */
      const ident = identOf(line);
      $$('[data-ident]', tr).forEach((el) => show(el, el.dataset.ident === ident));
      setText($('[data-cell="ident-derived"]', tr), displayLabel(line));
      setText($('[data-cell="detail"]', tr), kindNote(line));
      show($('[data-cell="detail"]', tr), !!kindNote(line));

      /* Quantity — derived rows read as derived and name the line they follow. */
      show($('[data-qty-typed]', tr), !line.qtyFrom);
      show($('[data-qty-derived]', tr), !!line.qtyFrom);
      setText($('[data-cell="derived-qty"]', tr), `${qtyFmt(effectiveQty(line, lines))} ${line.qtyUnit ?? ''}`.trim());
      setText($('[data-cell="derived-src"]', tr), line.qtyFrom ? `follows ${labelOfId(line.qtyFrom)}` : '');
      show($('[data-qty2]', tr), line.qty2 != null);
      show($('[data-qty2-add]', tr), line.qty2 == null && !line.qtyFrom && !unitLocked(line));

      /* A kind that already fixed the unit shows it as a value, not an input. */
      const locked = unitLocked(line);
      show($('[data-unit-open]', tr), !locked);
      show($('[data-unit-locked]', tr), locked);
      setText($('[data-unit-locked]', tr), lockedUnit(line));
      show($('[data-unit2-open]', tr), !locked);
      show($('[data-unit2-locked]', tr), locked);
      setText($('[data-unit2-locked]', tr), lockedUnit2(line));

      /* Travel — the whole chain is repainted, because raising a TRIP's occurrence
         count has to move every line under it at once. */
      if (line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL) {
        const k = travelKind(line.travelKind);
        setText($('[data-cell="travel-chain"]', tr), travelChain(line));
        setText($('[data-cell="travel-count-unit"]', tr), k?.countLabel ?? '');
        show($('[data-travel-duration]', tr), !!k?.durationLabel);
        setText($('[data-cell="travel-duration-unit"]', tr), k?.durationLabel ?? '');
        /* The kind decides where the rate comes from, so it decides which half of
           the rate cell is real. */
        show($('[data-travel-rate="gsa"]', tr), isScheduleRated(line));
        show($('[data-travel-rate="typed"]', tr), !isScheduleRated(line));
        setText($('[data-fact="travel-rate"]', tr), money(effectiveRate(line)));
      }

      /* Staffing months against the contract period — shown in both states. */
      const monthsWrap = $('[data-months-wrap]', tr);
      if (monthsWrap) {
        const tested = monthsTested(line);
        show(monthsWrap, tested);
        if (tested) {
          const bad = monthsOver(line);
          show($('[data-months="pass"]', tr), !bad);
          show($('[data-months="fail"]', tr), bad);
          setText($('[data-months-note]', tr), monthsText(line));
          const mErr = $('[data-months-err]', tr);
          show(mErr, bad);
          if (bad) setErrText(mErr, monthsErrText(line));
        }
      }

      /* Rate — shape B is a REFERENCED value, and the gate shows both verdicts. */
      setText($('[data-cell="ref-rate"]', tr), money(effectiveRate(line)));
      setText($('[data-cell="ref-src"]', tr), gsaSrcText(line));
      show($('[data-gsa="pass"]', tr), isScheduleRated(line) && !overridden(line));
      show($('[data-gsa="under"]', tr), overridden(line) && !over);
      show($('[data-gsa="over"]', tr), over);
      show($('[data-override-wrap]', tr), overridden(line));
      setBtnLabel($('[data-toggle-override]', tr)?.closest('.esa-button') ?? null,
        overridden(line) ? 'Use schedule rate' : 'Override rate');
      const gErr = $('[data-gsa-err]', tr);
      show(gErr, over);
      if (over) setErrText(gErr, gsaErrText(line));

      /* The capitalization threshold — a real gate, shown passing AND failing, and
         now condemning a misfiling in either direction. */
      const capWrap = $('[data-cap-wrap]', tr);
      if (capWrap) {
        const tested = thresholdTested(line);
        show(capWrap, tested);
        if (tested) {
          const pass = capPass(line, a);
          show($('[data-cap="pass"]', tr), pass);
          show($('[data-cap="fail"]', tr), !pass);
          /* The badge WORDING flips with the direction of the test — "below the
             threshold" is the pass in Supplies and the failure in Capital. */
          setBadgeText($('[data-cap="pass"]', tr), capPassText(line));
          setBadgeText($('[data-cap="fail"]', tr), capFailText(line));
          setText($('[data-cap-note]', tr), capText(line, a));
          const cErr = $('[data-cap-err]', tr);
          show(cErr, !pass);
          if (!pass) setErrText(cErr, capErrText(line, a));
          const move = $('[data-cap-move]', tr);
          show(move, !pass);
          setBtnLabel(move, capMoveText(line));
        }
      }

      /* Amount — typed only for a lump. */
      setText($('[data-cell="amount"]', tr), usd(a[line.id] ?? 0));

      /* Work element — unfinished must READ unfinished. */
      show($('[data-alloc-state="set"]', tr), sum === 100);
      show($('[data-alloc-state="unset"]', tr), sum !== 100);
      setBtnLabel($('[data-alloc-state="set"]', tr), allocSummary(line), allocTitle(line));
      setBtnLabel($('[data-alloc-state="unset"]', tr), allocSummary(line), allocTitle(line));
      setText($('[data-alloc-sum]', tr), `${sum}%`);
      const pErr = $('[data-alloc-panel-err]', tr);
      show(pErr, sum !== 100);
      if (sum !== 100) setErrText(pErr, allocErrText(line));
      const rErr = $('[data-alloc-row-err]', tr);
      show(rErr, sum !== 100);
      if (sum !== 100) setErrText(rErr, allocErrText(line));

      /* Inline background tint — never a colored left border. */
      const flag = over ? 'gsa'
        : sum !== 100 ? 'alloc'
        : !capPass(line, a) ? 'capital'
        : monthsOver(line) ? 'months'
        : '';
      if (flag) tr.dataset.flag = flag;
      else tr.removeAttribute('data-flag');

      /* The COR lens reads the same row as VALUES. Painted on every recompute so
         switching lenses mid-edit shows what the vendor actually just typed. */
      setText($('[data-fact="label"]', tr), displayLabel(line) || '(untitled line)');
      setText($('[data-fact="qty"]', tr), qtyFactText(line));
      show($('[data-fact="qty"]', tr), !line.qtyFrom);
      setText($('[data-fact="rate"]', tr), money(line.rate ?? 0));
      setText($('[data-fact="pct"]', tr), `${((line.pct ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(1)}%`);
      setText($('[data-fact="amount"]', tr), usd(a[line.id] ?? 0));
      const allocFact = $('[data-fact="alloc"]', tr);
      setText(allocFact, allocFactText(line));
      allocFact?.setAttribute('title', allocTitle(line));
    };

    const paintGroups = (priced: any) => {
      categories.forEach((cat: any) => {
        setText($(`[data-cat-total="${cat.key}"]`), usd(priced.byCategory[cat.key] ?? 0));
        (cat.blocks ?? []).forEach((b: any) => {
          const k = `${cat.key}:${b.key}`;
          setText($(`[data-block-total="${k}"]`), usd(priced.byBlock[k] ?? 0));
        });
      });
      /* Trip heads carry facts of their own — and the COR lens reads them as
         values, because a reviewer does not restructure someone else's itinerary. */
      trips.forEach((t: any) => {
        const head = $(`[data-trip="${t.key}"]`);
        if (!head) return;
        setText($('[data-trip-fact]', head), countOf(t.occurrences, 'trips'));
        setText($('[data-trip-cell="locality-note"]', head), localityNote(t));
      });
    };

    const paintTotals = (t: any) => {
      setText($('[data-total="direct"]'), usd(t.direct));
      setText($('[data-total="indirectBase"]'), usd(t.indirectBase));
      setText($('[data-total="indirect"]'), usd(t.indirect));
      setText($('[data-total="total"]'), usd(t.total));
      setText($('[data-indirect-rate]'), pct(cfg.rate));
      setText($('[data-indirect-ref]'), cfg.referenceDoc ?? '');
      setText($('[data-indirect-scope]'), scopeText(cfg));
      setText(
        $('[data-total-note]'),
        `${pct(t.pctOfValue, 1)} of the ${usd(contract.contractValue)} Contract Value · ${
          t.remaining >= 0 ? `${usd(t.remaining)} remaining` : `${usd(Math.abs(t.remaining))} over`
        }`,
      );
    };

    /** The one broadcast the reconciliation rail and the overhead block consume. */
    const fireTotals = (t: any) => {
      document.dispatchEvent(
        new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:totals', {
          detail: {
            total: t.total,
            direct: t.direct,
            indirect: t.indirect,
            indirectBase: t.indirectBase,
            remaining: t.remaining,
            pctOfValue: t.pctOfValue,
            failing: gsaFailingLines(lines).length,
            unallocated: unallocatedLines(lines).length,
            /* Both directions of the threshold now count — an item filed too high
               is as misfiled as one filed too low. */
            capitalFailing: thresholdMisfiled(lines).length,
            monthsFailing: monthsOverPeriod(lines).length,
          },
        }),
      );
    };

    /* The money moment: the rows that just moved because of THIS edit get a brief
       tint. dependentsOf() names exactly those — linked quantities and the
       shape-C lines computed off the edited line's staff group. */
    const flash = (els: (HTMLElement | null)[]) => {
      els.filter(Boolean).forEach((el) => {
        const node = el as HTMLElement;
        node.classList.remove('is-recomputed');
        void node.offsetWidth; // restart the animation on a repeat edit
        node.classList.add('is-recomputed');
        window.setTimeout(() => node.classList.remove('is-recomputed'), 1500);
      });
    };

    const recompute = (originId?: string) => {
      const priced = priceLines(lines, cfg);
      /* Shape-C base options follow the salary roster; only rebuild when it moves,
         so an open dropdown is never yanked out from under the vendor. */
      const sig = lines
        .filter((l) => l.category === 'personnel-salary')
        .map((l) => `${l.staffKey}:${l.label}`)
        .join('|');
      if (sig !== baseSig) {
        baseSig = sig;
        const opts = baseOptions();
        lines.forEach((l) => {
          const sel: any = rowOf(l.id) && $('[data-base-select]', rowOf(l.id)!);
          if (sel) { sel.options = opts; sel.value = baseValueOf(l); }
        });
      }
      lines.forEach((l) => paintRow(rowOf(l.id), l, priced));
      paintGroups(priced);
      paintTotals(priced.totals);
      fireTotals(priced.totals);
      if (originId) flash(dependentsOf(originId, lines).map((l: any) => rowOf(l.id)));
      return priced;
    };

    /* ── Wiring ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       esa-popover toggles on any click inside its anchor, and the slotted panel
       sits INSIDE that anchor — so a click on an allocation field would close the
       popover mid-edit. Stopping propagation at the panel keeps the edit alive
       (the popover's own outside-click check still passes, since the panel is a
       light-DOM child of the host). Known hub gap, logged in the summary. */
    const wireRow = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      $$('[data-alloc-panel]', tr).forEach((p) =>
        p.addEventListener('click', (ev) => ev.stopPropagation()),
      );

      /* The mark editor lives inside the same kind of popover, so it needs the
         same guard — and because the guard stops the bubble, its own Save and
         Remove are handled HERE rather than by the root delegate. */
      $$('[data-mark-panel]', tr).forEach((panel) =>
        panel.addEventListener('click', (ev) => {
          const t = ev.target as HTMLElement;
          if (t.closest('[data-mark-save]')) saveMark(tr);
          else if (t.closest('[data-mark-remove]')) removeMark(tr);
          ev.stopPropagation();
        }),
      );
    };

    /** Write (or rewrite) this row's mark from what the editor holds. */
    const saveMark = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;
      const sev = ($('[data-mark-sev-input]', tr) as any)?.value || SEVERITIES.REQUIRED;
      const reason = ($('[data-mark-reason-input]', tr) as any)?.value || 'other';
      const note = String(($('[data-mark-note-input]', tr) as any)?.value ?? '').trim();

      const flag = {
        lineId: line.id,
        severity: sev === SEVERITIES.QUESTION ? SEVERITIES.QUESTION : SEVERITIES.REQUIRED,
        reason,
        note,
        author: reviewMeta.reviewer,
        date: reviewMeta.today,
      };
      const i = (review.flags ?? []).findIndex((f: any) => f.lineId === line.id);
      if (i >= 0) review.flags[i] = flag;
      else review.flags = [...(review.flags ?? []), flag];
      /* Re-marking a line the vendor already ticked off puts it back on the list —
         the mark changed, so "addressed" is no longer a claim about this mark. */
      review.addressed = (review.addressed ?? []).filter((id: string) => id !== line.id);

      commitReview();
      closeMark(tr);
    };

    const removeMark = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;
      review.flags = (review.flags ?? []).filter((f: any) => f.lineId !== line.id);
      review.addressed = (review.addressed ?? []).filter((id: string) => id !== line.id);
      commitReview();
      fillMarkForm(tr, line);
      closeMark(tr);
    };

    /** esa-popover keeps `open` as a public reflected property. */
    const closeMark = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      const pop: any = $('[data-mark-popover]', tr);
      if (pop) pop.open = false;
    };

    $$('tr[data-line]').forEach(wireRow);
    lines.forEach((l) => {
      const tr = rowOf(l.id);
      if (tr) fillMarkForm(tr, l);
    });

    /* Selects are property-driven — seed the SSR rows once. There are five of them
       per row now (schedule row, percentage base, position, travel kind, vehicle
       kind), so this runs the same fillRow the added rows use rather than keeping
       a second, drifting list of what needs seeding. */
    lines.forEach((l) => {
      const tr = rowOf(l.id);
      if (tr) fillRow(tr, l);
    });
    baseSig = lines
      .filter((l) => l.category === 'personnel-salary')
      .map((l) => `${l.staffKey}:${l.label}`)
      .join('|');

    /* ── Add-line menus ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Every menu action is `category|block|shape|kind`. What the menu OFFERS is
       the vendor's vocabulary, not the model's: Vehicles lists the four vehicle
       kinds rather than "GSA schedule rate (B)" and "Quantity × rate (A)", and a
       trip lists the six travel kinds. Only where a category genuinely offers a
       structural choice with no better name — Supplies' itemize-or-lump — does
       the shape surface at all, and even then in plain words. */
    const SHAPE_MENU_LABEL: Record<string, string> = {
      [SHAPES.QTY_RATE]: 'Itemized — quantity × cost per unit',
      [SHAPES.AUTHORITY]: 'GSA schedule rate',
      [SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE]: 'Percentage of a base',
      [SHAPES.LUMP]: 'Lump sum',
      [SHAPES.TRAVEL]: 'Travel line',
    };
    $$('[data-add-menu]').forEach((menu: any) => {
      const cat = categories.find((c: any) => c.key === menu.dataset.addMenu);
      if (!cat) return;
      if (cat.key === 'vehicles') {
        menu.items = VEHICLE_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({
          label: k.label,
          action: `vehicles||${k.shape}|${k.key}`,
        }));
        return;
      }
      const blocks = cat.blocks ?? [null];
      menu.items = blocks.flatMap((b: any, bi: number) =>
        [
          ...(bi > 0 ? [{ divider: true }] : []),
          ...cat.shapes.map((s: string) => ({
            label: b ? `${b.label} — ${SHAPE_MENU_LABEL[s]}` : SHAPE_MENU_LABEL[s],
            action: `${cat.key}|${b ? b.key : ''}|${s}|`,
          })),
        ],
      );
    });

    /** A trip's own add menu: the six travel kinds, scoped to that trip. */
    const wireTravelMenu = (menu: any) => {
      menu.items = TRAVEL_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({
        label: k.label,
        action: `travel|${menu.dataset.addTravel}|${SHAPES.TRAVEL}|${k.key}`,
      }));
    };
    $$('[data-add-travel]').forEach(wireTravelMenu);


    /* ── Add a line INLINE — no modal. A new row is a real, editable row of the
       chosen shape, appended in the right group, focused and ready to type. It
       starts with allocations:{} — correctly unallocated, which moves the gate. */
    const insertRow = (tr: HTMLTableRowElement, catKey: string, block: string) => {
      const tbody = $(`tbody[data-cat="${catKey}"]`);
      if (!tbody) return;
      if (block) {
        const rows = $$(`tr[data-line][data-block="${block}"]`, tbody);
        const anchor = rows[rows.length - 1] ?? $(`tr[data-blockhead="${block}"]`, tbody);
        anchor?.after(tr);
        if (!anchor) tbody.appendChild(tr);
      } else {
        tbody.appendChild(tr);
      }
    };

    const defaultGsaKeyFor = (catKey: string) => {
      const sibling = lines.find((l) => l.category === catKey && l.gsaKey);
      /* Fall back within the category's OWN scope — gsaRates[0] is a travel row,
         which would seed a new vehicles line with a lodging rate. */
      return sibling?.gsaKey ?? gsaRatesFor(catKey)[0]?.key ?? gsaRates[0].key;
    };

    /** The first position not already on the budget — a new salary line should
        not open on a title the vendor already used. */
    const nextFreePosition = () => {
      const taken = new Set(lines.filter((l) => l.category === 'personnel-salary').map((l) => l.staffKey));
      return (positions.find((p: any) => !taken.has(p.key)) ?? positions[0]).key;
    };

    /** Seed the fields a KIND is responsible for. Called on add, and again every
        time the vendor changes the kind — the kind owns the units and the rate
        source, so changing it has to reset both rather than leave a lodging
        line's "nights" sitting on an airfare line. */
    const applyTravelKind = (line: any, kindKey: string) => {
      const k = travelKind(kindKey);
      line.travelKind = kindKey;
      if (!k) return;
      line.duration = k.durationLabel ? (line.duration ?? 1) : undefined;
      /* The kind decides WHAT is counted, so switching kind re-seeds the count
         from the last line in this trip counting the same unit — a lodging line
         turned into a mileage line must not keep "2" and silently mean vehicles. */
      line.count = seedCountFor(line.trip, kindKey, lines.filter((l) => l.id !== line.id));
      if (k.rateSource === 'gsa') {
        delete line.rate;
        const allowed = gsaRatesForTravelKind(kindKey);
        /* Default to the row for the trip's destination — resolving through the
           CONUS fallback when the place is not separately listed. */
        const match = gsaRowFor(kindKey, tripOf(line.trip)?.locality);
        if (!allowed.some((g: any) => g.key === line.gsaKey)) line.gsaKey = match?.key ?? '';
      } else {
        delete line.gsaKey;
        delete line.rateOverride;
        line.rate = line.rate ?? 0;
      }
      syncLabel(line);
    };

    const applyVehicleKind = (line: any, kindKey: string) => {
      const k = vehicleKind(kindKey);
      line.vehicleKind = kindKey;
      if (!k) return;
      line.shape = k.shape;
      line.qtyUnit = k.unit;
      if (k.secondUnit) { line.qty2 = line.qty2 ?? 1; line.qty2Unit = k.secondUnit; }
      else { delete line.qty2; delete line.qty2Unit; }
      if (k.shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY) {
        delete line.rate;
        const allowed = gsaRatesForVehicleKind(kindKey);
        if (!allowed.some((g: any) => g.key === line.gsaKey)) line.gsaKey = allowed[0]?.key ?? '';
      } else {
        delete line.gsaKey;
        delete line.rateOverride;
        line.rate = line.rate ?? 0;
      }
      if (!line.label) line.label = k.label;
    };

    const addLine = (catKey: string, block: string, shape: string, kind = '') => {
      const line: any = {
        id: `new-${++seq}-${Date.now().toString(36)}`,
        category: catKey,
        shape,
        label: '',
        allocations: {},
      };
      if (block) line.block = block;
      if (shape === SHAPES.QTY_RATE) { line.qty = 1; line.qtyUnit = ''; line.rate = 0; }
      if (shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY) { line.qty = 1; line.qtyUnit = ''; line.gsaKey = defaultGsaKeyFor(catKey); }
      if (shape === SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE) {
        const firstStaff = lines.find((l) => l.category === 'personnel-salary' && l.staffKey);
        line.base = firstStaff ? { type: 'staff', staffKey: firstStaff.staffKey } : { type: 'category', category: catKey };
        line.pct = 0;
      }
      if (shape === SHAPES.LUMP) line.amount = 0;
      if (catKey === 'personnel-salary') { line.staffKey = nextFreePosition(); line.qty = 1; line.qtyUnit = 'months'; }
      if (shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL) { line.trip = block; applyTravelKind(line, kind || TRAVEL_KINDS[0].key); }
      if (catKey === 'vehicles' && kind) applyVehicleKind(line, kind);
      syncLabel(line);

      const tpl = rowTemplates[line.shape];
      if (!tpl) return;
      const tr = tpl.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
      insertRow(tr, catKey, block);
      lines.push(line);
      fillRow(tr, line);
      /* A cloned row inherits the template row's mark markup — reset it, because
         a brand-new line carries no mark of its own. */
      fillMarkForm(tr, line);
      paintMark(tr, line);
      wireRow(tr);
      recompute();
      tr.classList.add('is-added');
      window.setTimeout(() => tr.classList.remove('is-added'), 1500);
      tr.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', behavior: 'smooth' });
      window.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        /* Focus whatever this category's FIRST question actually is — the free-text
           description on most rows, but the duration on a travel line, whose kind
           and locality the vendor already answered by choosing the menu item. */
        const first: any =
          line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL
            ? ($('[data-field="duration"]', tr) ?? $('[data-field="count"]', tr))
            : $('[data-field="label"]', tr);
        first?.shadowRoot?.querySelector('input')?.focus();
      });
      return tr;
    };

    /* ── A row that changes SHAPE has to be rebuilt ──────────────────────────
       Two edits do this: switching a vehicle kind between a schedule-rated lease
       and a typed premium, and moving a misfiled item across the capitalization
       threshold. Both mean the row's cells no longer exist in the same
       arrangement, so the honest move is to re-clone from the template for the
       new shape rather than to hide and reveal cells that were never there. */
    const rebuildRow = (line: any, catKey?: string, block?: string) => {
      const old = rowOf(line.id);
      const tpl = rowTemplates[line.shape];
      if (!old || !tpl) return;
      const tr = tpl.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
      old.remove();
      insertRow(tr, catKey ?? line.category, block ?? line.block ?? '');
      fillRow(tr, line);
      fillMarkForm(tr, line);
      paintMark(tr, line);
      wireRow(tr);
      recompute(line.id);
      flash([tr]);
      return tr;
    };

    /* ── Adding a TRIP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       A travel line with no trip has no locality, no travelers and no occurrence
       count to stand on, so Travel's category-level add makes the container, not
       a line. The head is cloned from a real trip head for the same reason rows
       are: the esa-* legos and the sub-block subtotal wiring come with it. */
    const tripHeadTemplate = ($('tr[data-trip]:not([data-trip=""])') as HTMLTableRowElement | null)
      ?.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement | undefined;

    const addTrip = () => {
      if (!tripHeadTemplate) return;
      const key = `trip-${++seq}-${Date.now().toString(36)}`;
      const trip: any = {
        key,
        locality: '',
        purpose: '',
        window: 'Dates to be set',
        occurrences: 1,
      };
      trips.push(trip);
      /* Travel's blocks ARE the trips, so the category's block list has to follow —
         paintGroups walks it to repaint sub-block subtotals. */
      const cat = categories.find((c: any) => c.key === 'travel');
      cat?.blocks?.push({ key, label: tripLabel(trip), trip });

      const head = tripHeadTemplate.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
      head.dataset.blockhead = key;
      head.dataset.trip = key;
      setText($('.cbf-lct__block-label', head), tripLabel(trip));
      setText($('[data-trip-cell="window"]', head), trip.window);
      const purpose: any = $('[data-trip-field="purpose"]', head);
      if (purpose) purpose.value = '';
      const totalCell = $('[data-block-total]', head);
      totalCell?.setAttribute('data-block-total', `travel:${key}`);
      setText(totalCell, usd(0));
      const dest: any = $('[data-trip-field="locality"]', head);
      if (dest) dest.value = '';
      const occ: any = $('[data-trip-field="occurrences"]', head);
      if (occ) occ.value = '1';
      setText($('[data-trip-cell="locality-note"]', head), localityNote(trip));
      const menu: any = $('[data-add-travel]', head);
      if (menu) { menu.dataset.addTravel = key; wireTravelMenu(menu); }

      $(`tbody[data-cat="travel"]`)?.appendChild(head);
      head.classList.add('is-added');
      window.setTimeout(() => head.classList.remove('is-added'), 1500);
      head.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', behavior: 'smooth' });
      recompute();
      return head;
    };

    root.addEventListener('menu-action', (e: any) => {
      const [catKey, block, shape, kind] = String(e.detail ?? '').split('|');
      if (catKey && shape) addLine(catKey, block, shape, kind ?? '');
    });

    /* ── Every esa-* control fires a composed `change`; delegate the lot ── */
    root.addEventListener('change', (e: any) => {
      const el = e.target as HTMLElement;

      /* A TRIP field belongs to the block head, not to any one row — and editing
         it moves every line under that trip at once, which is the entire reason
         travel is grouped this way. The trip objects are looked up by key inside
         the pricing engine, so they are updated in place rather than copied. */
      const head = el.closest?.('tr[data-trip]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (head && (el.dataset as any).tripField) {
        const trip: any = tripOf(head.dataset.trip!);
        if (!trip) return;
        const field = (el.dataset as any).tripField;
        const raw = (el as any).value;

        if (field === 'locality') {
          trip.locality = String(raw ?? '').trim();
          /* Re-point every schedule-rated line under this trip at the row for the
             new destination and its own kind — resolving through the CONUS
             fallback when the place is not separately listed. Leaving them on the
             old place's rate would be the quiet kind of wrong a reviewer catches
             months later. */
          lines
            .filter((l) => l.trip === trip.key && travelKind(l.travelKind)?.rateSource === 'gsa')
            .forEach((l) => {
              const match = gsaRowFor(l.travelKind, trip.locality);
              if (match) l.gsaKey = match.key;
              const sel: any = rowOf(l.id) && $('[data-gsa-select]', rowOf(l.id)!);
              if (sel) sel.value = l.gsaKey;
            });
          /* Say what the destination RESOLVED to. This is the thing the old
             locality dropdown hid by refusing unlisted places outright. */
          setText($('[data-trip-cell="locality-note"]', head), localityNote(trip));
        } else if (field === 'purpose') {
          trip.purpose = String(raw ?? '');
        } else {
          /* A journey happens at least once. */
          trip[field] = Math.max(1, num(raw));
        }

        setText($('.cbf-lct__block-label', head), tripLabel(trip));
        const moved = lines.filter((l) => l.trip === trip.key);
        moved.forEach(syncLabel);
        recompute();
        flash(moved.map((l) => rowOf(l.id)));
        return;
      }

      const tr = el.closest?.('tr[data-line]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (!tr) return;
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;
      const d = el.dataset;

      /* ── The identity pickers ── */
      if ('positionSelect' in d) {
        line.staffKey = (el as any).value;
        syncLabel(line);
        /* Fringe lines are keyed to a staff group, so renaming a salary line's
           position renames every fringe line computed off it. */
        lines.filter((l) => l.category === 'personnel-fringe').forEach(syncLabel);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
      if ('travelKindSelect' in d) {
        applyTravelKind(line, (el as any).value);
        fillRow(tr, line);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
      if ('vehicleKindSelect' in d) {
        const before = line.shape;
        applyVehicleKind(line, (el as any).value);
        /* Lease → insurance is a change of SHAPE, not just of values. */
        if (line.shape !== before) rebuildRow(line);
        else { fillRow(tr, line); recompute(line.id); }
        return;
      }

      if (d.field === 'label') {
        line.label = (el as any).value ?? '';
      } else if (d.field === 'qtyUnit' || d.field === 'qty2Unit') {
        line[d.field] = (el as any).value ?? '';
      } else if (d.field === 'pct') {
        line.pct = num((el as any).value) / 100;
      } else if (d.field) {
        line[d.field] = num((el as any).value);
      } else if (d.allocWe) {
        const p = num((el as any).value);
        if (p > 0) line.allocations[d.allocWe] = p;
        else delete line.allocations[d.allocWe];
      } else if ('gsaSelect' in d) {
        line.gsaKey = (el as any).value;
      } else if ('baseSelect' in d) {
        const [type, key] = String((el as any).value).split(':');
        line.base = type === 'staff' ? { type: 'staff', staffKey: key } : { type: 'category', category: key };
        /* A fringe line is NAMED by its base, so repointing it renames it. */
        syncLabel(line);
      } else {
        return;
      }
      recompute(line.id);
    });

    /* ── Clicks: add, delete, second quantity, rate override, subcontract seam ── */
    root.addEventListener('click', (e: any) => {
      const t = e.target as HTMLElement;

      if (t.closest?.('[data-add-trip]')) {
        addTrip();
        return;
      }

      const add = t.closest?.('[data-add-line]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (add) {
        const [catKey, block, shape, kind] = String(add.dataset.addLine).split('|');
        addLine(catKey, block, shape, kind ?? '');
        return;
      }

      const sheet = t.closest?.('[data-open-sheet]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (sheet) {
        document.dispatchEvent(
          new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:open-subcontract', {
            detail: { sheetId: sheet.dataset.openSheet, lineId: sheet.dataset.openSheetLine },
          }),
        );
        return;
      }

      const tr = t.closest?.('tr[data-line]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (!tr) return;
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;

      if (t.closest('[data-delete-line]')) {
        const i = lines.findIndex((l) => l.id === line.id);
        if (i >= 0) lines.splice(i, 1);
        tr.remove();
        recompute();
        return;
      }

      if (t.closest('[data-add-qty2]')) {
        line.qty2 = 1;
        line.qty2Unit = '';
        const f2: any = $('[data-field="qty2"]', tr);
        if (f2) f2.value = '1';
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }

      if (t.closest('[data-toggle-override]')) {
        if (line.rateOverride == null) line.rateOverride = gsaRate(line.gsaKey)?.rate ?? 0;
        else delete line.rateOverride;
        const f: any = $('[data-field="rateOverride"]', tr);
        if (f) f.value = numText(line.rateOverride);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }

      /* The threshold offers the MOVE, not just the verdict — the item is fine,
         it is only filed in the wrong category, and re-typing it there would lose
         its work-element split. */
      if (t.closest('[data-cap-move]')) {
        const target = capMoveTarget(line);
        line.category = target.category;
        if (target.block) line.block = target.block;
        else delete line.block;
        rebuildRow(line, target.category, target.block);
        return;
      }
    });

    /* ── The overhead block owns the rate AND its scope; this grid owns the base.
       Re-scoping must be visible HERE, so the indirect rows flash on receipt. ── */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:indirect-config', (e: any) => {
      const incoming = e.detail?.config;
      if (!incoming) return;
      cfg = { ...cfg, ...incoming, appliesTo: [...(incoming.appliesTo ?? cfg.appliesTo)] };
      recompute();
      flash($$('[data-indirect-row]'));
    });

    /* ── The review round trip ─────────────────────────────────────────────
       The panel owns the list and the decision; when either changes, the rows
       have to agree. Identity check first: this grid fires the same event, and
       repainting its own echo would be work for nothing. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:review', (e: any) => {
      const incoming = e.detail?.review;
      if (!incoming || incoming === review) return;
      review = incoming;
      lines.forEach((l) => {
        const tr = rowOf(l.id);
        if (tr) fillMarkForm(tr, l);
      });
      paintReview();
    });

    /* Switching lenses changes WHO is reading, which changes whether a mark is
       visible at all — so the marks repaint, and the read-only mirrors are
       already current from the last recompute. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-role:change', paintReview);

    /* "Go to line" from the review panel: bring the row into view and flash it,
       and — for the COR's Edit — open that row's mark editor, so the note is
       always written next to the numbers it is about. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:focus-line', (e: any) => {
      const { lineId, openFlag } = e.detail ?? {};
      const tr = lineId ? rowOf(lineId) : null;
      if (!tr) return;
      tr.scrollIntoView({ block: 'center', behavior: 'smooth' });
      flash([tr]);
      if (openFlag && role() === 'cor') {
        const pop: any = $('[data-mark-popover]', tr);
        if (pop) window.setTimeout(() => { pop.open = true; }, 250);
      }
    });

    /* Mount: paint everything, broadcast once, and re-broadcast on load so a
       sibling whose script initialises after this one still gets the state (the
       event is a pure snapshot, so repeating it is harmless). */
    paintReview();
    recompute();
    const rebroadcast = () => fireTotals(priceLines(lines, cfg).totals);
    if (document.readyState === 'complete') window.setTimeout(rebroadcast, 0);
    else window.addEventListener('load', rebroadcast, { once: true });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* Dense entry surface: 14px table base, 13px for the quiet second lines — and
     DARKENED as it shrinks (text-secondary, never a muted gray). The literal
     sizes are deliberate: the --type-size-* clamps bottom out at 11–12px on
     narrow viewports, which is under the 13px absolute floor. */
  .cbf-lct__lede {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__sr {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* overflow stays VISIBLE: the per-row allocation popover and the add-line shape
     menu position absolutely and cannot escape a clipping ancestor. So the table
     is sized to FIT its column instead of scrolling inside it — the page drops the
     reconciliation rail below the grid before the grid would ever need to scroll
     (--sidebar-content-min on the page). Horizontal scroll is the last resort at
     genuinely narrow widths, where a clipped popover is the lesser evil. */
  .cbf-lct__scroll {
    overflow: visible;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cbf-lct__scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  }

  .cbf-lct__table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 900px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* ── Per-category column captions ──
     Quieter than the old single table head, and deliberately so: it repeats nine
     times, and a full-weight header band at every category would read as nine
     tables stacked instead of one budget. It sits BELOW its category heading and
     above that category's rows, so it reads as a caption of the section it
     introduces rather than as a divider between sections. */
  .cbf-lct__colhead th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--spacing-150) var(--spacing-300);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .cbf-lct__colhead .cbf-lct__col-num { text-align: right; }
  .cbf-lct__col-span { min-width: 380px; }
  /* Column floors sum to ~1020px so the grid fits its column beside the rail on a
     laptop rather than overflowing it. */
  /* Six columns. The old "Rate source" column was dropped: what governs a rate
     (the GSA schedule row, the percentage base, the capitalization test) now
     lives in the Rate cell beside the number it produces, which is why Rate is
     the widest of the entry columns. Floors sum to ~900px. */
  .cbf-lct__col-line { min-width: 230px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-qty { min-width: 165px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-rate { min-width: 215px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-we { min-width: 140px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-act { width: 44px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-num { text-align: right; min-width: 115px; }

  .cbf-lct__cell {
    padding: var(--spacing-250) var(--spacing-300);
    vertical-align: top;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
  }
  .cbf-lct__cell--num {
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .cbf-lct__cell--act { text-align: right; }
  /* A shape whose cells genuinely don't exist collapses into one spanned cell —
     never a row of dead inputs. */
  .cbf-lct__cell--void { color: var(--color-text-secondary); }

  /* ── Category + sub-block header rows ── */
  .cbf-lct__cathead th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--spacing-300);
    background: var(--cbf-surface-section-head, var(--color-surface-sunken));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
  }
  .cbf-lct__cathead .cbf-lct__cell {
    background: var(--cbf-surface-section-head, var(--color-surface-sunken));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  .cbf-lct__cat-label {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__blockhead th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--spacing-200) var(--spacing-300) var(--spacing-200) var(--spacing-500);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
  }
  .cbf-lct__blockhead .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  .cbf-lct__block-label {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* ── Quiet second lines ── */
  .cbf-lct__note {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }
  /* "Nothing governs this rate" — a typed-rate line. Aligned with the controls in
     its sibling cells rather than floating at the cell's very top. */
  .cbf-lct__void-mark {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--form-height-md, 40px);
  }
  /* A value the vendor did NOT type — referenced or derived. Reads as a value,
     not as an input, and never as a disabled control. */
  .cbf-lct__ref,
  .cbf-lct__derived {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .cbf-lct__derived { font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular); }
  .cbf-lct__times {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    align-self: center;
  }

  /* ── Entry controls — one purpose per control, all md so a row lines up ──
     Fields keep the legos' default chrome (bordered, on surface). An earlier pass
     made them borderless-until-hover to quiet the grid; that buried the affordance
     — a vendor could not see where they were allowed to type until they clicked.
     Discoverability wins over visual quiet on an entry surface. Don't re-quiet
     these without solving the "where do I type" problem some other way. */
  .cbf-lct__field--label { width: 100%; }
  .cbf-lct__field--qty { width: 76px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--unit { width: 96px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--rate { width: 116px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--pct { width: 92px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--amount { width: 130px; margin-inline-start: auto; }
  .cbf-lct__field--trip { width: 116px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--dest { width: 230px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--purpose { width: 260px; }
  .cbf-lct__select { width: 100%; }

  /* A unit a KIND already decided is a value, not a control — it reads in line
     with the field it qualifies rather than as another empty box beside it. */
  .cbf-lct__unit {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    align-self: center;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ── Money ── */
  .cbf-lct__money {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }

  /* Until esa-popover upgrades it is an unknown element, so EVERY child renders
     inline — including slot="content". Without this guard each row shows its
     whole six-field allocation editor (and, in the COR lens, the mark form)
     stacked in the cell until hydration, which on a grid this size is a long and
     very loud flash. Same :not(:defined) guard the spoke uses on esa-tab-layout.
     If the script ever fails to load, this keeps the panels closed rather than
     dumping every editor onto the page. */
  esa-popover:not(:defined) [slot='content'] { display: none; }

  /* ── Allocation popover panel ── */
  .cbf-lct__alloc { width: 380px; }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-head {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-label {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-input { width: 104px; flex: none; }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-sum {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--spacing-150);
    margin: 0;
    padding-block-start: var(--spacing-200);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-sum strong {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* ── Row state: an inline background tint, NEVER a colored left border ── */
  tr[data-flag='gsa'] > .cbf-lct__cell,
  tr[data-flag='capital'] > .cbf-lct__cell,
  tr[data-flag='months'] > .cbf-lct__cell {
    background: var(--color-danger-subtle);
  }
  tr[data-flag='alloc'] > .cbf-lct__cell {
    background: var(--color-warning-subtle);
  }

  /* ── The money moment: rows that just recomputed because of an edit ──────────
     dependentsOf() names them exactly — a salary edit visibly moving its fringe
     line and the cell-phone line whose months follow it. */
  tr.is-recomputed > .cbf-lct__cell,
  tr.is-recomputed > th,
  tr.is-added > .cbf-lct__cell {
    animation: cbf-lct-flash 1.5s var(--easing-out) 1;
  }
  /* The flash LAYERS over the cell (inset shadow) rather than replacing its
     background-color — otherwise a flagged row loses its danger/warning tint for
     the duration of the flash and then snaps back to it. */
  @keyframes cbf-lct-flash {
    from { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax var(--color-info-subtle); }
    to { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax transparent; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    tr.is-recomputed > .cbf-lct__cell,
    tr.is-recomputed > th,
    tr.is-added > .cbf-lct__cell {
      animation: none;
      box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax var(--color-info-subtle);
    }
  }

  /* ── Totals block ── */
  .cbf-lct__totals th {
    text-align: right;
    padding: var(--spacing-250) var(--spacing-300);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__totals th .cbf-lct__note {
    display: block;
    max-width: none;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-regular);
  }
  .cbf-lct__totals .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .cbf-lct__totals tr:first-child th,
  .cbf-lct__totals tr:first-child .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
  }
  .cbf-lct__grand th,
  .cbf-lct__grand .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }
  .cbf-lct__grand .cbf-lct__cell strong { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  /* ── The two lenses ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     VENDOR sees entry controls and no mark editor. COR sees the same grid as
     values plus the mark editor, because a reviewer does not type into someone
     else's budget. Both renderings are in the markup; the document's role
     attribute reveals one, so switching lenses never loses an edit. */
  .cbf-lct__fact {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .cbf-lct__fact--label { font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium); }

  /* Vendor lens (the default): the mirrors and the mark editor stay out. */
  :global(html:not([data-cbf-role='cor'])) .cbf-lct__fact,
  :global(html:not([data-cbf-role='cor'])) [data-trip-fact],
  :global(html:not([data-cbf-role='cor'])) [data-mark-cell] {
    display: none !important;
